window.REALTEST_DATA = {"id":2,"title":"Đề kiểm tra 2","minutes":120,"questions":[{"id":1,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A financial services company is planning to establish a data mesh architecture that facilitates centralized data governance, analysis, and access control. The company has opted to utilize AWS Glue for managing data catalogs and conducting extract, transform, and load (ETL) operations.</p>\n\n<p>What combination of AWS services would be suitable to implement this data mesh effectively? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>Leverage AWS Data Exchange to globally share data and control access</p>","<p>Leverage AWS Glue DataBrew integration with AWS Glue Studio to orchestrate data sharing and control access</p>","<p>Choose Amazon S3 as the data storage service and leverage Amazon Athena for data analysis</p>","<p>Leverage AWS DataSync to globally share data and control access</p>","<p>Leverage AWS Lake Formation to centrally govern, secure, and globally share data</p>"],"correct":[2,4],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Choose Amazon S3 as the data storage service and leverage Amazon Athena for data analysis</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage AWS Lake Formation to centrally govern, secure, and globally share data</strong></p>\n\n<p>A data mesh architecture empowers business units (organized into domains) to have high ownership and autonomy for the technologies they use while providing technology that enforces data security policies both within and between domains through data sharing. Data consumers request access to these data products, which are approved by producer owners within a framework that provides decentralized governance, but centralized monitoring and auditing of the data sharing process.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Lake Formation helps you centrally govern, secure, and globally share data for analytics and machine learning. With Lake Formation, you can manage fine-grained access control for your data lake data on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and its metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.</p>\n\n<p>Lake Formation tag-based access control (TBAC) solves the role explosion problem by allowing data stewards to create LF-tags (based on their business needs) that are attached to resources. You can create policies on a smaller number of logical tags instead of specifying policies on named resources. LF-tags enable you to categorize and explore data based on taxonomies, which reduces policy complexity and scales permissions management. You can create and manage policies with tens of logical tags instead of thousands of resources.</p>\n\n<p>Lake Formation TBAC decouples policy creation from resource creation, which helps data stewards manage permissions on many databases, tables, and columns by removing the need to update policies every time a new resource is added to the data lake. Finally, TBAC allows you to create policies even before the resources come into existence. All you have to do is tag the resource with the right LF-tag to make sure existing policies manage it.</p>\n\n<p>Here is a deep-dive into data mesh implementation using Lake Formation with a case study:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/</a></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage AWS DataSync to globally share data and control access</strong> - AWS DataSync is an online data movement and discovery service that simplifies and accelerates data migrations to AWS as well as moving data to and from on-premises storage, edge locations, other cloud providers, and AWS Storage services. This option acts as a distractor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage AWS Data Exchange to globally share data and control access</strong> - AWS Data Exchange is a service that makes it easy for customers to find, subscribe to, and use third-party data in the AWS Cloud. This option acts as a distractor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage AWS Glue DataBrew integration with AWS Glue Studio to orchestrate data sharing and control access</strong> - AWS Glue DataBrew is a fully managed visual data preparation service for cleaning, normalizing, and transforming data. AWS Glue DataBrew is integrated with AWS Glue Studio, so you can orchestrate DataBrew recipes within your AWS Glue ETL jobs and workflows. You cannot use these services to implement a data mesh.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/data-mesh/\">https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/data-mesh/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/</a></p>"},{"id":2,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>An Amazon Redshift cluster is used to store sensitive information of a business-critical application. The regulatory guidelines mandate tracking audit logs of the Redshift cluster. The business needs to store the audit logs securely by encrypting the logs at rest. The logs are to be stored for a year at least and audits need to be conducted on the audit logs on a monthly basis.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is a cost-effective solution that fulfills the requirement of storing the logs securely while having access to the logs for monthly audits?</p>","answers":["<p>Enable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits</p>","<p>Enable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Copy the data into the Amazon Redshift cluster from Amazon S3 when data needs to be queried for monthly audits</p>","<p>Enable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits</p>","<p>Enable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon QuickSight to query the data for monthly audits</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits</strong></p>\n\n<p>Audit logging is not turned on by default in Amazon Redshift. When you turn on logging on your cluster, Amazon Redshift creates and uploads logs to Amazon S3 that capture data from the time audit logging is enabled to the present time. Each logging update is a continuation of the information that was already logged.</p>\n\n<p>Audit logging to Amazon S3 is an optional, manual process. When you enable logging on your cluster, you are enabling logging to Amazon S3 only. Currently, you can only use Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift Spectrum is a feature of Amazon Redshift that lets you run queries against your data lake in Amazon S3, with no data loading or ETL required. When you issue an SQL query, it goes to the Amazon Redshift endpoint, which generates and optimizes a query plan. Amazon Redshift determines what data is local and what is in Amazon S3, generates a plan to minimize the amount of S3 data that needs to be read, and requests Amazon Redshift Spectrum workers out of a shared resource pool to read and process data from S3.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/77cf4bb8f4d46ffadc3ce6c624024687d2e60529003fe834c0ed0c6a27766b73.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/77cf4bb8f4d46ffadc3ce6c624024687d2e60529003fe834c0ed0c6a27766b73.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Copy the data into the Amazon Redshift cluster from Amazon S3 when data needs to be queried for monthly audits</strong> - Copying data into the Redshift cluster for enabling monthly audits would turn out to be a costly solution. Using Redshift Spectrum is the right fit for the given scenario.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon QuickSight to query the data for monthly audits</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits</strong></p>\n\n<p>Currently, you can only use Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Hence, both these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html</a></p>"},{"id":3,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A research agency has deployed two autonomous underwater vehicles in the ocean to track parameters such as salinity, temperature, speed and direction of currents, etc. Vehicle A has twenty sensors whereas Vehicle B has ten sensors. Each sensor is identified by a unique ID. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is being used to gather data from each sensor. A single Amazon Kinesis Data Stream with two shards is configured based on the total incoming and outgoing data throughput. Two partition keys are generated based on the name of the vehicles. During initial testing, data from Vehicle A experiences a bottleneck whereas data from Vehicle B does not. The overall stream throughput has been validated to be less than the assigned Kinesis Data Streams throughput.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following solutions would you use to address this bottleneck without increasing the total cost and complexity of the system?</p>","answers":["<p>Set up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle A with twenty shards and then direct Vehicle A sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream</p>","<p>Increase the number of shards in Kinesis Data Streams to support throughput from both vehicles</p>","<p>Change the partition key to use the sensor ID instead of the name of the vehicle</p>","<p>Set up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle B with ten shards and then direct Vehicle B sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Change the partition key to use the sensor ID instead of the name of the vehicle</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is a fully managed, serverless streaming data service that makes it easy to elastically ingest and store logs, events, clickstreams, and other forms of streaming data in real time. Kinesis Data Streams has two capacity modes: on-demand and provisioned, and both come with specific billing options.</p>\n\n<p>In the on-demand mode, pricing is based on the volume of data ingested and retrieved along with a per-hour charge for each data stream in your account.</p>\n\n<p>With provisioned capacity mode, you specify the number of shards necessary for your application based on its write and read request rate. A shard is a unit of capacity that provides 1 MB/second of write and 2 MB/second of read throughout. A record is the data that your data producer adds to your Amazon Kinesis data stream. A PUT Payload Unit is counted in 25 KB payload “chunks” that comprise a record. For example, a 5 KB record contains one PUT Payload Unit, a 45 KB record contains two PUT Payload Units, and a 1 MB record contains 40 PUT Payload Units. PUT Payload Unit is charged a per-million PUT Payload Units rate. In the provisioned mode (applicable for the given scenario), you pay by the shard hour (1MB/second ingress, 2MB/second egress) and PUT Payload Units, per 1,000,000 units.</p>\n\n<p>For the existing use case, the Kinesis Data Stream has been partitioned into two shards, one for each of the two vehicles. Therefore, data from the 20 sensors of Vehicle A is going to the first shard and the data from the 10 sensors of Vehicle B is going to the other shard, causing an imbalance and thereby resulting in a throughput bottleneck for Vehicle A. The correct solution is to partition the shards on the basis of the sensor ID which will result in an even distribution across the two shards. The use-case already states that the overall stream throughput is less than the assigned Kinesis Data Streams throughput, so there is no need to provision extra shards.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Increase the number of shards in Kinesis Data Streams to support throughput from both vehicles</strong> - As mentioned in the explanation above, there is no need to provision extra shards as it would result in additional costs. The right solution is to partition the shards on the basis of the sensor ID which will result in an even distribution across the two shards already provisioned.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle A with twenty shards and then direct Vehicle A sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle B with ten shards and then direct Vehicle B sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream</strong></p>\n\n<p>Both these options have been added as distractors. There is no need to provision extra shards for any of the vehicles, as it would result in additional costs. The right solution is to partition the shards on the basis of the sensor ID which will result in an even distribution across the two shards already provisioned.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/pricing/\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/pricing/</a></p>"},{"id":4,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A pharmaceutical company uses AWS Glue jobs to process research datasets stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company organizes the datasets by S3 prefixes that represent sensitivity levels such as public, internal, confidential, and regulated. The security team must allow each AWS Glue job role to access only the prefixes that match the job's approved data classification. The team must also restrict access to an approved business-hours window from 8 AM to 6 PM.</p>\n\n<p>Which elements must the company include in a custom IAM policy to meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use a Condition element with the s3:prefix condition key to restrict all object access and use aws:CurrentTime with DateGreaterThanEquals and DateLessThanEquals operators to restrict the access window</p>","<p>Use a Resource element with S3 object ARN patterns that include wildcards for each approved prefix and use a Condition element with aws:CurrentTime and DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan operators</p>","<p>Use a Condition element with the s3:ResourceAccount condition key to restrict bucket ownership and add an explicit Deny statement outside the approved business-hours window</p>","<p>Use a Resource element with S3 bucket ARN patterns for each classification prefix and use a Condition element with aws:RequestTag to restrict access during the approved business-hours window</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use a Resource element with S3 object ARN patterns that include wildcards for each approved prefix and use a Condition element with aws:CurrentTime and DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan operators</strong></p>\n\n<p>An IAM policy can restrict Amazon S3 object-level permissions by listing specific S3 object ARNs in the Resource element. To grant access only to approved prefixes, the policy can use object ARN patterns such as arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/confidential/* or arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/regulated/*.</p>\n\n<p>The policy can restrict the time window by using the AWS global condition key aws:CurrentTime with date condition operators such as DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan. This allows the security team to limit when requests from the AWS Glue job role can be authorized.</p>\n\n<p>This solution matches both requirements. The Resource element scopes S3 access to the relevant classification prefixes, and the Condition element enforces time-based access by evaluating the request time during IAM authorization.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use a Condition element with the s3:ResourceAccount condition key to restrict bucket ownership and add an explicit Deny statement outside the approved business-hours window</strong> - The s3:ResourceAccount condition key helps restrict access based on the AWS account that owns the S3 resource. This can be useful in some cross-account access control scenarios.</p>\n\n<p>However, this condition key does not restrict access to specific classification prefixes inside a bucket. The company needs prefix-specific object ARNs or prefix-aware list conditions, not only an account ownership check.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use a Condition element with the s3:prefix condition key to restrict all object access and use aws:CurrentTime with DateGreaterThanEquals and DateLessThanEquals operators to restrict the access window</strong> - The s3:prefix condition key can restrict prefixes for bucket listing operations such as s3:ListBucket. It is useful when a policy must control which keys a principal can list in an S3 bucket.</p>\n\n<p>However, s3:prefix does not, by itself, restrict object-level actions such as s3:GetObject or s3:PutObject. The policy still needs S3 object ARN patterns in the Resource element to restrict actual object access to the approved prefixes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use a Resource element with S3 bucket ARN patterns for each classification prefix and use a Condition element with aws:RequestTag to restrict access during the approved business-hours window</strong> - The Resource element must use correct S3 ARN formats. Bucket-level ARNs such as arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket apply to bucket-level actions, while object-level actions require object ARNs such as arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/prefix/*.</p>\n\n<p>The aws:RequestTag condition key evaluates tags that a request passes to a supported service action. It does not provide a general mechanism to restrict S3 object access to business hours.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security_iam_service-with-iam.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security_iam_service-with-iam.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/amazon-s3-policy-keys.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/amazon-s3-policy-keys.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_resource.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_resource.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition_operators.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition_operators.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/techniques-for-writing-least-privilege-iam-policies/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/techniques-for-writing-least-privilege-iam-policies/</a></p>"},{"id":5,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A financial services company runs an Amazon RDS for MySQL database that stores customer transaction records. The compliance team must collect audit logs that identify database users who modify transaction data. The team must also track users or roles that make administrative changes to the RDS DB instance. The audit records must be available for review and investigation.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Enable Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the DB instance, create metric filters for database modifications and instance changes, and configure notifications for suspicious database activity</p>","<p>Configure Amazon RDS Performance Insights to capture SQL activity, enable enhanced monitoring for operating system metrics, store the monitoring output in CloudWatch Logs, and use CloudWatch dashboards to identify users who changed transaction rows and RDS instance settings</p>","<p>Create an Amazon EventBridge rule to monitor Amazon RDS events, invoke an AWS Lambda function to parse the events, and store the parsed records in Amazon OpenSearch Service</p>","<p>Enable native Amazon RDS for MySQL database audit logging, publish the audit logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, configure CloudWatch metric filters and alarms, and enable AWS CloudTrail logging for RDS API activity</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable native Amazon RDS for MySQL database audit logging, publish the audit logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, configure CloudWatch metric filters and alarms, and enable AWS CloudTrail logging for RDS API activity</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon RDS for MySQL supports database audit logging by using the MariaDB Audit Plugin. The audit plugin can record database-level activity, including the database user and the operations that the user performs. This meets the requirement to track each user who modifies data in the database.</p>\n\n<p>Publishing the database audit logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs makes the logs available for centralized retention, search, monitoring, and audit review. CloudWatch metric filters and alarms can help the company identify important audit patterns, such as unexpected write activity or sensitive table changes.</p>\n\n<p>AWS CloudTrail complements the database audit logs by recording Amazon RDS API calls. CloudTrail helps identify users, roles, timestamps, and API actions for administrative changes to the DB instance, such as modifying DB settings, changing logging configuration, or updating instance properties. Together, native database audit logs and CloudTrail cover both database data modifications and RDS instance-level changes.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon EventBridge rule to monitor Amazon RDS events, invoke an AWS Lambda function to parse the events, and store the parsed records in Amazon OpenSearch Service</strong> - Amazon EventBridge can receive Amazon RDS events and route those events to targets such as Lambda. RDS events can help monitor operational events, such as failovers, backups, maintenance, or configuration-related notifications.</p>\n\n<p>However, RDS events do not provide a complete database audit trail that identifies each database user who modifies data inside MySQL tables. This option also does not use native MySQL audit logging, which is required for database-level user activity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the DB instance, create metric filters for database modifications and instance changes, and configure notifications for suspicious database activity</strong> - CloudWatch Logs can store logs that Amazon RDS publishes, and metric filters can detect patterns in log streams. Notifications can help alert operations teams when suspicious patterns appear.</p>\n\n<p>However, simply enabling CloudWatch Logs and metric filters does not create the required audit source. The company must first enable native RDS for MySQL database audit logging to capture database user activity, and it must use CloudTrail to audit RDS instance-level API changes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure Amazon RDS Performance Insights to capture SQL activity, enable enhanced monitoring for operating system metrics, store the monitoring output in CloudWatch Logs, and use CloudWatch dashboards to identify users who changed transaction rows and RDS instance settings</strong> - Amazon RDS Performance Insights helps analyze database performance and SQL load. Enhanced Monitoring provides operating system metrics for the DB instance, such as CPU, memory, process, and file system activity.</p>\n\n<p>These tools do not provide a compliance audit trail of each database user who modifies data, and they do not capture complete RDS administrative API activity. The company needs native database audit logging for MySQL activity and CloudTrail for RDS instance changes.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Appendix.MySQL.Options.AuditPlugin.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Appendix.MySQL.Options.AuditPlugin.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_LogAccess.MySQLDB.PublishtoCloudWatchLogs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_LogAccess.MySQLDB.PublishtoCloudWatchLogs.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/logging-using-cloudtrail.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/logging-using-cloudtrail.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/amazon-rds-monitoring-alerting/audit-trails.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/amazon-rds-monitoring-alerting/audit-trails.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/monitor-amazon-rds-for-mysql-and-mariadb-logs-with-amazon-cloudwatch/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/monitor-amazon-rds-for-mysql-and-mariadb-logs-with-amazon-cloudwatch/</a></p>"},{"id":6,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>The data engineering team at a company is moving the static content from the company's logistics website hosted on Amazon EC2 instances to an Amazon S3 bucket. The team wants to use an Amazon CloudFront distribution to deliver the static content. The security group used by the Amazon EC2 instances allows the website to be accessed by a limited set of IP ranges from the company's suppliers. Post-migration to Amazon CloudFront, access to the static content should only be allowed from the aforementioned IP addresses.</p>\n\n<p>Which options would you combine to build a solution to meet these requirements? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>Create a new security group that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new security group with the Amazon CloudFront distribution</p>","<p>Configure an origin access control (OAC) and associate it with the Amazon CloudFront distribution. Set up the permissions in the Amazon S3 bucket policy so that only the OAC can read the objects</p>","<p>Create an AWS WAF ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution</p>","<p>Create a new NACL that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new NACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution</p>","<p>Create an AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon S3 bucket policy</p>"],"correct":[1,2],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure an origin access control (OAC) and associate it with the Amazon CloudFront distribution. Set up the permissions in the Amazon S3 bucket policy so that only the OAC can read the objects</strong></p>\n\n<p>When you use Amazon CloudFront with an Amazon S3 bucket as the origin, you can configure Amazon CloudFront and Amazon S3 in a way that provides the following benefits:</p>\n\n<p>Restricts access to the Amazon S3 bucket so that it's not publicly accessible</p>\n\n<p>Makes sure that viewers (users) can access the content in the bucket only through the specified Amazon CloudFront distribution—that is, prevents them from accessing the content directly from the bucket, or through an unintended CloudFront distribution.</p>\n\n<p>To do this, configure Amazon CloudFront to send authenticated requests to Amazon S3, and configure Amazon S3 to only allow access to authenticated requests from Amazon CloudFront. Amazon CloudFront provides two ways to send authenticated requests to an Amazon S3 origin via origin access control (OAC).</p>\n\n<p>Exam Alert:</p>\n\n<p>You should note that AWS recommends using OAC because it supports:</p>\n\n<p>All Amazon S3 buckets in all AWS Regions, including opt-in Regions launched after December 2022</p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 server-side encryption with AWS KMS (SSE-KMS)</p>\n\n<p>Dynamic requests (POST, PUT, etc.) to Amazon S3</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an AWS WAF ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS WAF is a web application firewall that lets you monitor the HTTP and HTTPS requests that are forwarded to your protected web application resources. You can protect the following resource types:</p>\n\n<p>Amazon CloudFront distribution</p>\n\n<p>Amazon API Gateway REST API</p>\n\n<p>Application Load Balancer</p>\n\n<p>AWS AppSync GraphQL API</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Cognito user pool</p>\n\n<p>AWS WAF also lets you control access to your content. Based on conditions that you specify, such as the IP addresses that requests originate from or the values of query strings, your protected resource responds to requests either with the requested content, with an HTTP 403 status code (Forbidden), or with a custom response.</p>\n\n<p>If you want to allow or block web requests based on the IP addresses that the requests originate from, create one or more IP match conditions via your AWS WAF. An IP match condition lists up to 10,000 IP addresses or IP address ranges that your requests originate from.</p>\n\n<p>For the given use case, you should add those IP addresses that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group into the IP match condition.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon S3 bucket policy</strong> - You cannot associate an AWS WAF ACL with an Amazon S3 bucket policy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a new NACL that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new NACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution</strong> - NACL is associated with a subnet within a VPC. Amazon CloudFront delivers your content through a worldwide network of data centers called edge locations. So a NACL cannot be associated with an Amazon CloudFront distribution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a new security group that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new security group with the Amazon CloudFront distribution</strong> - A security group acts as a virtual firewall for your Amazon EC2 instances to control incoming and outgoing traffic. Inbound rules control the incoming traffic to your instance, and outbound rules control the outgoing traffic from your instance. Amazon CloudFront delivers your content through a worldwide network of data centers called edge locations. So a security group cannot be associated with Amazon CloudFront distribution.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-s3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-s3.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/what-is-aws-waf.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/what-is-aws-waf.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/classic-web-acl-ip-conditions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/classic-web-acl-ip-conditions.html</a></p>"},{"id":7,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A financial services company has recently migrated from on-premises infrastructure to AWS Cloud. The data engineering team wants to implement a solution that allows all resource configurations to be reviewed and make sure that they meet compliance guidelines. Also, the solution should be able to offer the capability to look into the resource configuration history across the application stack.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following solutions would you recommend to the team?</p>","answers":["<p>Use AWS Config to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes</p>","<p>Use Amazon CloudWatch to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes</p>","<p>Use AWS Systems Manager to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes</p>","<p>Use AWS CloudTrail to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Config to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Config is a service that enables you to assess, audit, and evaluate the configurations of your AWS resources. With Config, you can review changes in configurations and relationships between AWS resources, dive into detailed resource configuration histories, and determine your overall compliance against the configurations specified in your internal guidelines. You can use Config to answer questions such as - “What did my AWS resource look like at xyz point in time?”</p>\n\n<p>How AWS Config Works:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/44f6c566f85b623c587a8bc0acc92a8a1385a1dcd860bb7f90ec76712da22519.png\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/44f6c566f85b623c587a8bc0acc92a8a1385a1dcd860bb7f90ec76712da22519.png\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/config/\">https://aws.amazon.com/config/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon CloudWatch to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes</strong> - AWS CloudWatch provides you with data and actionable insights to monitor your applications, respond to system-wide performance changes, optimize resource utilization, and get a unified view of operational health. You cannot use Amazon CloudWatch to maintain a history of resource configuration changes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS CloudTrail to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes</strong> - With AWS CloudTrail, you can log, continuously monitor and retain account activity related to actions across your AWS infrastructure. You can use AWS CloudTrail to answer questions such as - “Who made an API call to modify this resource?”. AWS CloudTrail provides an event history of your AWS account activity thereby enabling governance, compliance, operational auditing, and risk auditing of your AWS account. You cannot use AWS CloudTrail to maintain a history of resource configuration changes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Systems Manager to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes</strong> - Using AWS Systems Manager, you can group resources, like Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon S3 buckets, or Amazon RDS instances, by application, view operational data for monitoring and troubleshooting, and take action on your groups of resources. You cannot use AWS Systems Manager to maintain a history of resource configuration changes.</p>\n\n<p>Exam Alert:</p>\n\n<p>You may see scenario-based questions asking you to select one of Amazon CloudWatch vs AWS CloudTrail vs AWS Config. Just remember the following -</p>\n\n<p>Think resource performance monitoring, events, and alerts; think Amazon CloudWatch.</p>\n\n<p>Think account-specific activity and audit; think AWS CloudTrail.</p>\n\n<p>Think resource-specific history, audit, and compliance; think AWS Config.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/config/\">https://aws.amazon.com/config/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/\">https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail/\">https://aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/\">https://aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/</a></p>"},{"id":8,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>An online grocery company stores same-day purchase transactions in Amazon Redshift. The company has a sales_orders table that contains the existing order records. The company also has a daily_updates staging table that contains newly created orders and changed order records from the last processing window.</p>\n\n<p>The company must remove outdated records from the sales_orders table and load the latest versions from the daily_updates table. Several reporting applications query the sales_orders table throughout the day and must display current order information.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to delete outdated records from the sales_orders table and insert the latest records from the daily_updates table</p>","<p>Create an Amazon Redshift stored procedure that deletes matching stale records from the sales_orders table and inserts the latest records from the daily_updates staging table</p>","<p>Use Amazon Athena federated queries to read outdated records from the Amazon Redshift sales_orders table, delete those records, and insert the latest rows from the daily_updates table</p>","<p>Create an Amazon Redshift materialized view that joins the sales_orders table with the daily_updates table and configure downstream applications to query the materialized view instead of refreshing the base table</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon Redshift stored procedure that deletes matching stale records from the sales_orders table and inserts the latest records from the daily_updates staging table</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift stored procedures can encapsulate SQL logic that runs inside the database. A stored procedure can include data manipulation language operations such as DELETE and INSERT, which makes it suitable for refreshing an existing Redshift table from a staging table.</p>\n\n<p>The stored procedure can delete rows from the sales_orders table that match records in the daily_updates staging table. After the delete step completes, the procedure can insert the latest records from the staging table into the sales_orders table. This pattern keeps the downstream applications pointed to the same sales_orders table while ensuring that the table contains current records.</p>\n\n<p>This solution also keeps the data movement inside Amazon Redshift. The company does not need to unload data to Amazon S3, use Athena for table maintenance, or introduce external processing services for a standard Redshift staging-table update pattern.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2bfa6157468dab4ea93ed3ba2adede0aa2d87bd5c38fa82d32b9cf798691ad6b.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/2bfa6157468dab4ea93ed3ba2adede0aa2d87bd5c38fa82d32b9cf798691ad6b.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/bringing-your-stored-procedures-to-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/bringing-your-stored-procedures-to-amazon-redshift/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena federated queries to read outdated records from the Amazon Redshift sales_orders table, delete those records, and insert the latest rows from the daily_updates table</strong> - Amazon Athena federated queries allow Athena to query data from sources other than Amazon S3 through data source connectors. However, Athena federated queries are not the right mechanism to perform transactional maintenance on an Amazon Redshift table.</p>\n\n<p>This option adds an unnecessary query layer outside Redshift. The requirement is to update a Redshift table from a Redshift staging table, and Redshift can perform the delete and insert operations directly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to delete outdated records from the sales_orders table and insert the latest records from the daily_updates table</strong> - Amazon Redshift Spectrum allows Redshift to query external data in Amazon S3. It is useful when users need to analyze data in a data lake without loading all the data into Redshift tables.</p>\n\n<p>This requirement involves two Redshift tables and requires changes to the managed sales_orders table. Redshift Spectrum does not provide the appropriate mechanism to delete stale rows from an internal Redshift table and insert current rows from a staging table.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon Redshift materialized view that joins the sales_orders table with the daily_updates table and configure downstream applications to query the materialized view instead of refreshing the base table</strong> - A materialized view can improve query performance for precomputed query results. However, this option does not remove stale rows from the sales_orders table or insert the latest rows into the table.</p>\n\n<p>The requirement specifically states that downstream applications need the sales_orders table to display up-to-date information. Redirecting applications to a materialized view changes the access pattern and leaves the base table unresolved.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_updating-inserting-using-staging-tables-.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_updating-inserting-using-staging-tables-.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_PROCEDURE.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_PROCEDURE.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/bringing-your-stored-procedures-to-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/bringing-your-stored-procedures-to-amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":9,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A financial services company needs to implement a transaction review workflow. Each payment request must pass through several dependent validation stages, including fraud review, balance verification, compliance screening, and final approval. Some validations call external systems and can take several hours to return a result.</p>\n\n<p>The company must approve or reject every payment request within 18 hours. The complete workflow must run for less than 18 hours and must wait for each validation result before moving to the next stage. The company wants the solution with the least operational cost.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Create a Standard workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern to wait for each validation stage to finish</p>","<p>Use Lambda functions to implement the workflow and use EventBridge rules to invoke each validation stage after the preceding stage finishes</p>","<p>Create an Express workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern so each external validation step can return its result</p>","<p>Create an Express workflow in Step Functions and split each transaction into multiple executions so no single execution exceeds the workflow duration limit</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a Standard workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern to wait for each validation stage to finish</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Step Functions Standard workflows are designed for durable, auditable workflows that can run for long periods. A Standard workflow can run for up to one year, so an 18-hour transaction validation process fits within the supported duration.</p>\n\n<p>The Wait for Callback pattern is appropriate when a workflow must pause until an external validation system returns a result. Step Functions can pass a task token to the validation process and wait until the external system calls back with success or failure before the workflow continues to the next dependent stage.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational cost and overhead for the stated requirement because Step Functions manages state, sequencing, retries, timeouts, and workflow history. The company does not need to build custom state tracking, callback handling, or orchestration logic across multiple services.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2c458c42e396f94a92b2b448fc614b3d8f91a24b84897315c36b2a661cbe8e8b.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/2c458c42e396f94a92b2b448fc614b3d8f91a24b84897315c36b2a661cbe8e8b.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/choosing-workflow-type.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/choosing-workflow-type.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Express workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern so each external validation step can return its result</strong> - Express workflows are optimized for high-volume, short-duration workflows. They can be cost-effective for brief event-processing workloads that do not require long execution time or callback integration.</p>\n\n<p>This option does not meet the requirement because Express workflows can run for only up to 5 minutes and do not support the <code>.waitForTaskToken</code> callback integration pattern. The transaction workflow can last many hours and must wait for external validation callbacks.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Lambda functions to implement the workflow and use EventBridge rules to invoke each validation stage after the preceding stage finishes</strong> - Lambda and EventBridge can coordinate simple event-driven processing. A validation function could publish an event that starts the next validation stage.</p>\n\n<p>The issue with this approach is that the company would need to build custom workflow state management, timeout handling, retries, ordering guarantees, and final approval or rejection logic. Step Functions Standard provides these orchestration capabilities as a managed service.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Express workflow in Step Functions and split each transaction into multiple executions so no single execution exceeds the workflow duration limit</strong> - Splitting work across multiple Express workflow executions can sometimes help with short-lived high-volume tasks. A parent or external coordinator can start new executions for different workflow stages.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because it creates custom coordination to work around Express workflow limits. It also does not provide native callback waiting for long-running external validations, so it is less suitable than one Standard workflow using callback tasks.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/choosing-workflow-type.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/choosing-workflow-type.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-to-resource.html#connect-wait-token\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-to-resource.html#connect-wait-token</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/callback-task-sample-sqs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/callback-task-sample-sqs.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/building-cost-effective-aws-step-functions-workflows/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/building-cost-effective-aws-step-functions-workflows/</a></p>"},{"id":10,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A data engineer is configuring an AWS Step Function for a banking system workflow. The workflow must process large amounts of data files in parallel and it should simultaneously apply transformations for each file.</p>\n\n<p>Which Step Functions state is the right fit for this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Concurrent state</p>","<p>Parallel state</p>","<p>Choice state</p>","<p>Map state</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Map state</strong></p>\n\n<p>You can use the Map state to run a set of workflow steps for each item in a dataset. The Map state's iterations run in parallel, which makes it possible to process a dataset quickly. Map states can use a variety of input types, including a JSON array, a list of Amazon S3 objects, or a CSV file.</p>\n\n<p>Step Functions provides two types of processing modes for using the Map state in your workflows: inline mode and distributed mode.</p>\n\n<p>By default, Map states run in inline mode. In inline mode, the Map state accepts only a JSON array as input. In this mode, the Map state supports up to 40 concurrent iterations.</p>\n\n<p>In distributed mode, the Map state allows high-concurrency processing. In distributed mode, the Map state processes the items in the dataset in iterations called child workflow executions. You can specify the number of child workflow executions that can run in parallel. Each child workflow execution has its own, separate execution history from that of the parent workflow. If you don't specify, Step Functions runs 10,000 parallel child workflow executions in parallel.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Parallel state</strong> - The Parallel state (\"Type\": \"Parallel\") can be used to add separate branches of execution in your state machine. A <code>Parallel</code> state causes AWS Step Functions to execute each branch, starting with the state named in that branch's <code>StartAt</code> field, as concurrently as possible, and wait until all branches terminate (reach a terminal state) before processing the <code>Parallel</code> state's <code>Next</code> field.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Choice state</strong> - A Choice state (\"Type\": \"Choice\") adds conditional logic to a state machine. This state contains an array of Choice Rules that determine which state the state machine transitions to next. You use a comparison operator in a Choice Rule to compare an input variable with a specific value. For example, using Choice Rules you can compare if an input variable is greater than or less than 100.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Concurrent state</strong> - This state does not exist. This is a made-up option that acts as a distractor.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/amazon-states-language-map-state.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/amazon-states-language-map-state.html</a></p>"},{"id":11,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A data analytics company measures what the consumers watch and what advertising they’re exposed to. This real-time data is ingested into its on-premises data center and subsequently, the daily data feed is compressed into a single file and uploaded on Amazon S3 for backup. The typical compressed file size is around 2 gigabytes.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is the fastest way to upload the daily compressed file into Amazon S3?</p>","answers":["<p>Upload the compressed file using multipart upload</p>","<p>Upload the compressed file using multipart upload with Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration (Amazon S3TA)</p>","<p>Upload the compressed file in a single operation</p>","<p>FTP the compressed file into an Amazon EC2 instance that runs in the same region as the Amazon S3 bucket. Then transfer the file from the Amazon EC2 instance into the Amazon S3 bucket</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Upload the compressed file using multipart upload with Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration (Amazon S3TA)</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration (Amazon S3TA) enables fast, easy, and secure transfers of files over long distances between your client and an S3 bucket. Transfer Acceleration takes advantage of Amazon CloudFront’s globally distributed edge locations. As the data arrives at an edge location, data is routed to Amazon S3 over an optimized network path.</p>\n\n<p>Multipart upload allows you to upload a single object as a set of parts. Each part is a contiguous portion of the object's data. You can upload these object parts independently and in any order. If transmission of any part fails, you can retransmit that part without affecting other parts. After all parts of your object are uploaded, Amazon S3 assembles these parts and creates the object. If you're uploading large objects over a stable high-bandwidth network, use multipart uploading to maximize the use of your available bandwidth by uploading object parts in parallel for multi-threaded performance. If you're uploading over a spotty network, use multipart uploading to increase resiliency to network errors by avoiding upload restarts.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Upload the compressed file in a single operation</strong> - In general, when your object size reaches 100 megabytes, you should consider using multipart uploads instead of uploading the object in a single operation. Multipart upload provides improved throughput - you can upload parts in parallel to improve throughput. Therefore, this option is not correct.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Upload the compressed file using multipart upload</strong> - Although using multipart upload would certainly speed up the process, combining it with Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration (Amazon S3TA) would further improve the transfer speed. Therefore just using multipart upload is not the correct option.</p>\n\n<p><strong>FTP the compressed file into an Amazon EC2 instance that runs in the same region as the Amazon S3 bucket. Then transfer the file from the Amazon EC2 instance into the Amazon S3 bucket</strong> -  This is a roundabout process of getting the file into Amazon S3 and added as a distractor. Although it is technically feasible to follow this process, it would involve a lot of scripting and certainly would not be the fastest way to get the file into Amazon S3.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/transfer-acceleration.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/transfer-acceleration.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/uploadobjusingmpu.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/uploadobjusingmpu.html</a></p>"},{"id":12,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A life sciences company runs a shared Amazon EMR cluster on Amazon EC2 instances. Several analytics teams use the cluster to run interactive analysis and batch transformations through EMR Studio workspaces and EMR steps.</p>\n\n<p>The teams need to submit EMR steps that run Apache Spark jobs and read reference data from Amazon DynamoDB tables. One DynamoDB table contains confidential clinical trial data that only the clinical research team can access. A data engineer must make sure that only the clinical research team can access the confidential DynamoDB table from jobs that run on the shared EMR cluster.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Set up runtime roles for EMR steps and allow only the clinical research team to submit steps with a runtime role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table</p>","<p>Configure IAM roles for EMRFS requests and map the clinical research team to a role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table</p>","<p>Create separate EMR Studio workspaces for each analytics team and rely on workspace separation to prevent unauthorized Spark jobs from reading the confidential DynamoDB table</p>","<p>Configure AWS Lake Formation permissions for the clinical research team and grant the team access to the confidential DynamoDB table through LF-Tags</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up runtime roles for EMR steps and allow only the clinical research team to submit steps with a runtime role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table</strong></p>\n\n<p>Runtime roles for EMR steps are designed for job-scoped access control on Amazon EMR clusters. A data engineer can define an IAM runtime role that has permissions to read the confidential DynamoDB table and allow only the clinical research team to submit EMR steps with that role.</p>\n\n<p>This approach is important in a multi-tenant EMR cluster. Without runtime roles, jobs can inherit broad permissions from the cluster instance profile, which can allow teams to access AWS resources that they should not use. Runtime roles narrow the permissions to the job or step that is running.</p>\n\n<p>This solution meets the requirement because the confidential DynamoDB access is tied to the team-authorized runtime role, not to the entire shared cluster. Other teams can continue to use EMR Studio workspaces and EMR steps, but their steps run with roles that do not include permissions for the confidential table.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure AWS Lake Formation permissions for the clinical research team and grant the team access to the confidential DynamoDB table through LF-Tags</strong> - AWS Lake Formation can provide fine-grained permissions for data lake resources that use the AWS Glue Data Catalog, such as catalog databases, tables, columns, and supported S3-backed datasets. It can also integrate with some Amazon EMR access control patterns.</p>\n\n<p>However, the confidential data is in a DynamoDB table, and the teams need EMR steps to run Spark jobs that fetch data from DynamoDB. Lake Formation permissions are not the direct control for DynamoDB table access in this scenario.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure IAM roles for EMRFS requests and map the clinical research team to a role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table</strong> - IAM roles for EMRFS requests can help isolate Amazon S3 access by mapping users or groups to IAM roles for EMRFS operations. This is useful when teams need different permissions for S3 data accessed through EMRFS.</p>\n\n<p>However, EMRFS role mapping is specific to EMRFS access patterns and does not control Spark access to DynamoDB tables. The requirement is to restrict DynamoDB access for EMR steps, so runtime roles are the appropriate job-scoped control.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create separate EMR Studio workspaces for each analytics team and rely on workspace separation to prevent unauthorized Spark jobs from reading the confidential DynamoDB table</strong> - EMR Studio workspaces can separate user experiences and provide managed notebook environments for teams. This can improve organization and usability for interactive analytics.</p>\n\n<p>However, workspace separation alone does not enforce DynamoDB table permissions for Spark jobs that run on the shared cluster. The job needs a scoped IAM role that determines which AWS resources the job can access.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-steps-runtime-roles.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-steps-runtime-roles.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-studio-runtime.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-studio-runtime.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-access-iam.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-access-iam.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-iam-roles.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-iam-roles.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-runtime-roles-for-amazon-emr-steps-use-iam-roles-and-aws-lake-formation-for-access-control-with-amazon-emr/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-runtime-roles-for-amazon-emr-steps-use-iam-roles-and-aws-lake-formation-for-access-control-with-amazon-emr/</a></p>"},{"id":13,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A financial analytics company is configuring an AWS Glue job to read encrypted data files from an Amazon S3 bucket. The data engineer has configured an AWS Glue connection that uses a private subnet and has attached an IAM role with the required S3 permissions. When the data engineer runs the job, AWS Glue returns an error that indicates a problem with the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint for the selected subnet.</p>\n\n<p>The data engineer must resolve the connectivity issue so that the AWS Glue job can access the S3 bucket.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Create an interface VPC endpoint for AWS Glue, enable private DNS for the endpoint, and attach the interface endpoint to the same subnet that the AWS Glue job uses</p>","<p>Verify that the route table associated with the AWS Glue job subnet includes a route to the Amazon S3 prefix list that targets the S3 gateway endpoint</p>","<p>Update the AWS Glue job code so the S3 connection details use a fully qualified domain name for the bucket endpoint</p>","<p>Update the AWS Glue security group to allow inbound traffic from the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Verify that the route table associated with the AWS Glue job subnet includes a route to the Amazon S3 prefix list that targets the S3 gateway endpoint</strong></p>\n\n<p>An Amazon S3 gateway endpoint works by adding a route to the route table that is associated with the subnet. The route uses the Amazon S3 prefix list as the destination and the S3 gateway endpoint as the target.</p>\n\n<p>When an AWS Glue job uses a connection that places the job in a VPC subnet, the subnet must have a valid network path to Amazon S3. If the job cannot validate or use the S3 gateway endpoint, the data engineer should verify that the route table for the selected subnet is associated with the correct S3 gateway endpoint route.</p>\n\n<p>This solution directly addresses the reported endpoint issue. IAM permissions and bucket policies control authorization, but the error indicates that AWS Glue cannot reach Amazon S3 through the VPC gateway endpoint path. Correcting the subnet route table fixes the network path that the job needs to access the S3 bucket.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Update the AWS Glue security group to allow inbound traffic from the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint</strong> - Security groups control traffic to and from elastic network interfaces. Amazon S3 gateway endpoints do not use endpoint network interfaces in the same way that interface endpoints do.</p>\n\n<p>Because an S3 gateway endpoint is a route table target, adding inbound security group rules from the endpoint does not resolve the routing problem. The data engineer must verify the subnet route table instead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an interface VPC endpoint for AWS Glue, enable private DNS for the endpoint, and attach the interface endpoint to the same subnet that the AWS Glue job uses</strong> - An interface endpoint for AWS Glue provides private connectivity to AWS Glue APIs from a VPC. It does not provide private connectivity from an AWS Glue job to Amazon S3 objects.</p>\n\n<p>The requirement involves an Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint. Creating an AWS Glue interface endpoint does not add the required S3 route to the subnet route table and does not fix the S3 data access path.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Update the AWS Glue job code so the S3 connection details use a fully qualified domain name for the bucket endpoint</strong> - AWS Glue jobs can read S3 paths without requiring the job code to include a fully qualified S3 service domain name. The application code is not the main issue when the failure identifies the S3 VPC gateway endpoint.</p>\n\n<p>This option does not address the subnet routing configuration. The data engineer needs to ensure that the VPC subnet used by the AWS Glue connection has a route to Amazon S3 through the gateway endpoint.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/vpc-endpoints-s3.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connection-S3-VPC.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connection-S3-VPC.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/start-connecting.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/start-connecting.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/gateway-endpoints.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/gateway-endpoints.html</a></p>"},{"id":14,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A smart factory company runs a streaming data pipeline on AWS to process telemetry from industrial IoT devices. The pipeline uses Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest machine readings and Amazon Data Firehose to deliver the processed records to an Amazon S3 bucket. A data engineer must monitor the health of the pipeline and receive alerts when ingestion throughput, downstream consumption, or delivery to Amazon S3 shows abnormal behavior. The solution must require the least operational effort.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Deploy Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to analyze the telemetry stream, detect unusual sensor patterns, and invoke alerts when machine readings deviate from expected ranges</p>","<p>Configure CloudWatch alarms for key Kinesis Data Streams and Data Firehose metrics, including IncomingBytes, OutgoingBytes, and DeliveryToS3.Success</p>","<p>Create a Lambda function that runs every day, calls AWS APIs to inspect the Kinesis stream and Firehose delivery stream, and sends SNS notifications</p>","<p>Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket and review object-level API activity to determine whether Data Firehose delivered telemetry files successfully</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure CloudWatch alarms for key Kinesis Data Streams and Data Firehose metrics, including IncomingBytes, OutgoingBytes, and DeliveryToS3.Success</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon CloudWatch is the native monitoring service for both Kinesis Data Streams and Amazon Data Firehose. Kinesis Data Streams publishes metrics such as IncomingBytes and OutgoingBytes, which help the data engineer understand whether data producers and consumers are behaving as expected.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Data Firehose also publishes CloudWatch metrics for delivery behavior, including DeliveryToS3.Success for Amazon S3 destinations. Monitoring this metric helps the company confirm whether Firehose successfully delivers records to the S3 bucket.</p>\n\n<p>CloudWatch alarms provide the least operational effort because the company can configure thresholds and notifications directly on managed service metrics. The data engineer does not need to build custom polling code, manually inspect logs, run SQL reconciliation jobs, or deploy a stream processing application only for pipeline health monitoring.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a Lambda function that runs every day, calls AWS APIs to inspect the Kinesis stream and Firehose delivery stream, and sends SNS notifications</strong> - A Lambda function could call AWS APIs, inspect stream status, and publish notifications through Amazon SNS. This design can work if the company builds all required checks and alert logic.</p>\n\n<p>However, this approach creates custom monitoring code that the team must deploy, test, schedule, and maintain. CloudWatch already receives the relevant metrics from Kinesis Data Streams and Data Firehose, so custom daily checks add unnecessary operational overhead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Deploy Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to analyze the telemetry stream, detect unusual sensor patterns, and invoke alerts when machine readings deviate from expected ranges</strong> - Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is well suited for real-time stream analytics and stateful event processing. It can detect anomalies in the actual telemetry values if the business needs operational analytics on machine behavior.</p>\n\n<p>However, the requirement is to monitor the health of the streaming pipeline, not to analyze sensor data patterns. Deploying a Flink application only to monitor pipeline service health is more complex than using CloudWatch alarms on service metrics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket and review object-level API activity to determine whether Data Firehose delivered telemetry files successfully</strong> - AWS CloudTrail can record API activity, and S3 data events can provide object-level visibility for supported S3 actions. This can help with auditing access to objects in a bucket.</p>\n\n<p>However, CloudTrail is not the best tool for monitoring streaming pipeline health. It does not provide a simple alarm-based view of Kinesis ingestion throughput, consumer output, or Firehose delivery success.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch-metrics.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch-metrics.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/AlarmThatSendsEmail.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/AlarmThatSendsEmail.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch-logs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch-logs.html</a></p>"},{"id":15,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>An IT company has built a custom data warehousing solution for a retail organization by using Amazon Redshift. As part of the cost optimizations, the company wants to move any historical data (any data older than a year) into Amazon S3, as the daily analytical reports consume data for just the last year. However, the analysts want to retain the ability to cross-reference this historical data along with the daily reports.</p>\n\n<p>The company wants to develop a solution with the LEAST amount of effort and MINIMUM cost. Which option would you recommend to facilitate this use-case?</p>","answers":["<p>Use AWS Glue ETL job to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift</p>","<p>Use the Amazon Redshift COPY command to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Amazon Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift</p>","<p>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to create Amazon Redshift cluster tables pointing to the underlying historical data in Amazon S3. The analytics team can then query this historical data to cross-reference with the daily reports from Redshift</p>","<p>Setup access to the historical data via Amazon Athena. The analytics team can run historical data queries on Amazon Athena and continue the daily reporting on Amazon Redshift. In case the reports need to be cross-referenced, the analytics team needs to export these in flat files and then do further analysis</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to create Amazon Redshift cluster tables pointing to the underlying historical data in Amazon S3. The analytics team can then query this historical data to cross-reference with the daily reports from Redshift</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift is a fully-managed petabyte-scale cloud-based data warehouse product designed for large-scale data set storage and analysis.</p>\n\n<p>Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift Spectrum resides on dedicated Amazon Redshift servers that are independent of your cluster. The Redshift Spectrum pushes many compute-intensive tasks, such as predicate filtering and aggregation, down to the Redshift Spectrum layer. Thus, Amazon Redshift Spectrum queries use much less of your cluster's processing capacity than other queries.</p>\n\n<p>Redshift Spectrum Overview:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Setup access to the historical data via Amazon Athena. The analytics team can run historical data queries on Amazon Athena and continue the daily reporting on Amazon Redshift. In case the reports need to be cross-referenced, the analytics team needs to export these in flat files and then do further analysis</strong> - Amazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.\nProviding access to historical data via Athena would mean that historical data reconciliation would become difficult as the daily report would still be produced via Redshift. Such a setup is cumbersome to maintain on a day-to-day basis. Hence the option to use Athena is ruled out.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use the Amazon Redshift COPY command to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Amazon Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue ETL job to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift</strong></p>\n\n<p>Loading historical data into Amazon Redshift via COPY command or AWS Glue ETL job would cost heavily for a one-time ad-hoc process. The same result can be achieved more cost-efficiently by using Amazon Redshift Spectrum. Therefore both these options to load historical data into Redshift are also incorrect for the given use-case.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html#c-spectrum-overviewhttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html#c-spectrum-overview\nhttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://ifg.udemy.com/course/practice-exams-aws-certified-data-engineer-associate-r/learn/#\">amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/</a></p>"},{"id":16,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>The data engineering team at an e-commerce company is doing a root-cause analysis for a recent spike in CPU utilization for an RDS MySQL DB instance that caused the application to perform poorly. The standard metrics available in Amazon CloudWatch are not enough to guide the investigation. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to determine what caused the CPU spike.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following steps would you recommend to provide more details about the underlying processes and queries resulting in an increase in the CPU load? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon Athena to analyze the SQL statements being run on the RDS instance</p>","<p>Activate Enhanced Monitoring to view CPU utilization information for the RDS MySQL DB instance</p>","<p>Activate Amazon CloudWatch Events and review the event data that has the SQL statements behind the CPU spikes</p>","<p>Enable RDS Performance Insights and review the appropriate dashboard to visualize the database load and filter the load by waits, SQL statements, hosts, or users</p>","<p>Implement ElastiCache in front of the RDS DB instance to reduce the CPU load on the RDS instance</p>"],"correct":[1,3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p>Monitoring is an important part of maintaining the reliability, availability, and performance of Amazon RDS. You can monitor network throughput, client connections, I/O for read, write, or metadata operations, and burst credit balances for your DB instances. AWS provides various tools that you can use to monitor Amazon RDS.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8b84d4243c70b1012263f33862f64b8164be4553649bda45a86c48e10d5462ad.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/8b84d4243c70b1012263f33862f64b8164be4553649bda45a86c48e10d5462ad.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/3919fc78ca9b19bef939984bbc43d2913d4dfcdf8611120b4ce4f8d02b0469d6.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/3919fc78ca9b19bef939984bbc43d2913d4dfcdf8611120b4ce4f8d02b0469d6.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Activate Enhanced Monitoring to view CPU utilization information for the RDS MySQL DB instance</strong></p>\n\n<p>You can use Enhanced Monitoring to capture metrics in real-time for the operating system (OS) that your DB instance runs on. Enhanced Monitoring metrics are useful for the given use case as it allows you to see how the different processes or threads on a DB instance use the CPU. By default, Enhanced Monitoring metrics are stored for 30 days in the CloudWatch Logs, which are different from typical CloudWatch metrics.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/53221a8ba628577928440f0d6625ae698f2df2d065bc5453ef463ed009c40589.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/53221a8ba628577928440f0d6625ae698f2df2d065bc5453ef463ed009c40589.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_Monitoring.OS.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_Monitoring.OS.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable RDS Performance Insights and review the appropriate dashboard to visualize the database load and filter the load by waits, SQL statements, hosts, or users</strong></p>\n\n<p>Performance Insights allows you to understand your database's performance and help you analyze any issues that affect it. With the Performance Insights dashboard, you can visualize the database load and filter the load by waits, SQL statements, hosts, or users.</p>\n\n<p>You can use Performance Insights for the given use case as it allows you to see the underlying SQL statements causing an increase in the database load.</p>\n\n<p>The central metric for Performance Insights is DB Load, which represents the average number of active sessions for the DB engine. The DB Load metric is collected every second. An active session is a connection that has submitted work to the DB engine and is waiting for a response. For example, if you submit a SQL query to the DB engine, the database session is active while the DB engine is processing the query.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/14cb7e1fbee4594c29c95a73903c695ae432ff0bc2465f31fc6db79fcf9fee9b.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/14cb7e1fbee4594c29c95a73903c695ae432ff0bc2465f31fc6db79fcf9fee9b.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Implement ElastiCache in front of the RDS DB instance to reduce the CPU load on the RDS instance</strong> - You can apply a band-aid on the underlying issue by using ElastiCache as it can potentially decrease the CPU load on the RDS DB instance, however, it will present no insights about the underlying processes and queries resulting in an increase in the CPU load.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena to analyze the SQL statements being run on the RDS instance</strong> - Amazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.</p>\n\n<p>Recently Athena has introduced federated queries that can be used to quickly analyze records from different sources such as RDS MySQL. However, you still cannot use it to analyze the SQL statements being run on the RDS instance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Activate Amazon CloudWatch Events and review the event data that has the SQL statements behind the CPU spikes</strong> - This is a made-up option as CloudWatch Events do not contain SQL statements in the event data.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_Monitoring.OS.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_Monitoring.OS.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.html</a></p>"},{"id":17,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A company uses Amazon Redshift as its enterprise data warehouse. A data engineer must query a table named analytics.fulfilled_order_history, which contains 120 columns. The query must return all columns except the columns named customer_account_id and source_system_key.</p>\n\n<p>Which Amazon Redshift SQL statement will meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>SELECT * FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history WHERE column_name NOT IN ('customer_account_id', 'source_system_key');</p>","<p>SELECT * NOT IN customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;</p>","<p>SELECT * EXCLUDE customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;</p>","<p>SELECT * EXCEPT customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>SELECT * EXCLUDE customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift supports the EXCLUDE column_list clause with SELECT *. This syntax returns all columns from the table except the columns that the query explicitly names in the EXCLUDE list.</p>\n\n<p>The data engineer needs to query a wide table with 120 columns and omit only customer_account_id and source_system_key. SELECT * EXCLUDE customer_account_id, source_system_key avoids listing the other 118 columns manually.</p>\n\n<p>This statement directly meets the requirement because it uses valid Amazon Redshift SQL syntax and applies the exclusion at the projection level. The query returns the same rows from analytics.fulfilled_order_history while removing only the two specified columns from the result set.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>SELECT * NOT IN customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;</strong> - Amazon Redshift does not use SELECT * NOT IN to exclude columns from the SELECT list. NOT IN is a predicate that SQL commonly uses in WHERE clauses to compare values against a list.</p>\n\n<p>This statement treats column exclusion as if it were a row-filtering construct. It does not provide valid Redshift syntax for returning all columns except selected columns.</p>\n\n<p><strong>SELECT * EXCEPT customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;</strong> - EXCEPT is not the correct Redshift syntax for excluding columns from SELECT *. In SQL, EXCEPT generally represents a set operation that returns rows from one query that do not appear in another query.</p>\n\n<p>This option resembles syntax that exists in some SQL dialects for column exclusion, but the Amazon Redshift syntax for this requirement is EXCLUDE column_list. Therefore, this statement will not meet the requirement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>SELECT * FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history WHERE column_name NOT IN ('customer_account_id', 'source_system_key');</strong> - A WHERE clause filters rows, not columns. The expression shown attempts to compare a metadata-like column_name value to a list of column names, but the query still does not change the set of columns returned by SELECT *.</p>\n\n<p>Even if the table had a column named column_name, this statement would filter rows based on values in that column. It would not exclude customer_account_id and source_system_key from the result set.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_EXCLUDE_list.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_EXCLUDE_list.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SELECT_synopsis.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SELECT_synopsis.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SELECT_list.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SELECT_list.html</a></p>"},{"id":18,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A company stores its user information in a MySQL database table called <code>user</code>. The <code>name</code> column has the users' names stored in <code>firstname lastname</code> format. Due to legacy reasons, a few users have the names stored in <code>lastname firstname</code> format. A data engineer has been tasked with developing a query that returns all records where the <code>name</code> column has values starting with <code>John</code> or <code>Doe</code>, on a case-insensitive basis.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following queries represents the correct solution?</p>","answers":["<p><code>SELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * '$(John|Doe)'</code></p>","<p><code>SELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ '^(John|Doe)'</code></p>","<p><code>SELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * '^(John|Doe)'</code></p>","<p><code>SELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ '$(John|Doe)'</code></p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong><code>SELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * '^(John|Doe)'</code></strong></p>\n\n<p>In SQL, <code>~</code> is the regular expression operator. You can use <code>~*</code> to make the query case-insensitive. The <code>^</code> operator matches a pattern at the start of a string. For the given use case, you can use the <code>^</code> operator to find all records where the <code>name</code> column has values starting with <code>John</code> or <code>Doe</code> on a case-insensitive basis.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong><code>SELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * '$(John|Doe)'</code></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><code>SELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ '$(John|Doe)'</code></strong></p>\n\n<p>The <code>$</code> operator matches a pattern at the end of a string. So, both these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong><code>SELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ '^(John|Doe)'</code></strong> - Without the <code>~ *</code> characters combination in the WHERE clause, the query would return the results on a case-sensitive basis, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://www.atlassian.com/data/sql/how-regex-works-in-sql\">https://www.atlassian.com/data/sql/how-regex-works-in-sql</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/regexp.html\">https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/regexp.html</a></p>"},{"id":19,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A data engineer needs to run a lightweight transformation whenever a user uploads a report file to an Amazon S3 bucket. Each transformation completes in less than 45 seconds. After the transformation finishes, the data engineer must receive the output in an email message. Users upload approximately one report file every hour throughout the day.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?</p>","answers":["<p>Create a Lambda function for the transformation, configure S3 Event Notifications to invoke the function on object creation, and publish the output to an SNS topic with an email subscription</p>","<p>Deploy an EMR cluster with EMRFS to read the S3 files, schedule transformation steps, write output to another S3 bucket, and configure S3 notifications to an SNS topic</p>","<p>Create an AWS Glue job that runs every hour, scans the S3 bucket for new report files, transforms matching files, and writes email-ready output to Amazon S3</p>","<p>Run an ECS task that polls the S3 bucket for new report files, executes the transformation code on a schedule, and sends email messages through operating system commands</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a Lambda function for the transformation, configure S3 Event Notifications to invoke the function on object creation, and publish the output to an SNS topic with an email subscription</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Lambda is the best fit because the transformation runs for less than 45 seconds and occurs only about once per hour. Lambda can run the transformation code without requiring the company to provision or manage servers.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 Event Notifications can invoke the Lambda function automatically when a new object is created in the bucket. This event-driven approach avoids polling and starts the transformation only when a user uploads a file.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon SNS can deliver messages to email subscribers. The Lambda function can publish the transformed output to an SNS standard topic, and the data engineer can subscribe an email address to receive the result. This creates a simple serverless workflow with minimal operational effort.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run an ECS task that polls the S3 bucket for new report files, executes the transformation code on a schedule, and sends email messages through operating system commands</strong> - Amazon ECS can run containerized transformation code, and scheduled tasks can process files in Amazon S3. This can be useful when workloads need custom runtime packaging or long-running containers.</p>\n\n<p>The issue with this approach is that polling adds unnecessary work for a low-volume event-driven workload. The team must manage task definitions, scheduling, container images, polling logic, and email commands when S3 can invoke Lambda directly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an AWS Glue job that runs every hour, scans the S3 bucket for new report files, transforms matching files, and writes email-ready output to Amazon S3</strong> - AWS Glue can run ETL jobs over S3 data and can be appropriate for larger transformations, data preparation, and scheduled processing.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that the job would scan for new files on a schedule instead of reacting to each upload event. For a sub-minute transformation, Lambda with S3 Event Notifications is simpler and more operationally efficient.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Deploy an EMR cluster with EMRFS to read the S3 files, schedule transformation steps, write output to another S3 bucket, and configure S3 notifications to an SNS topic</strong> - Amazon EMR can process large-scale distributed workloads and can access S3 data through EMRFS. It is useful for Spark, Hive, Presto, and other big data frameworks.</p>\n\n<p>This does not match the workload size or duration. A short transformation that runs once per hour does not justify provisioning and operating an EMR cluster, and S3 notifications to SNS alone would not run the transformation code.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-event-types-and-destinations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-event-types-and-destinations.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/s3-event-notification/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/s3-event-notification/</a></p>"},{"id":20,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A digital media company processes 650 GB of audience engagement and campaign delivery data every day. The company stores incoming CSV files in Amazon S3, and the schemas are registered in AWS Glue Data Catalog. The company needs to convert the CSV files to Apache Parquet format and write the output to another S3 bucket.</p>\n\n<p>The pipeline must run two long-running conversion and enrichment processes concurrently. Each process requires at least 15 GiB of memory. A third correlation process must begin only after the first two processes finish successfully. The company wants the solution with the least operational overhead.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Step Functions to orchestrate multiple Lambda functions, and configure the third Lambda function to run after the first two functions finish</p>","<p>Use AWS Glue workflows to run the first two AWS Glue Spark jobs in parallel, and start the third AWS Glue job after both jobs finish</p>","<p>Use AWS Batch to run three containerized Spark jobs on managed compute environments, and use DynamoDB to track when the first two jobs finish</p>","<p>Use Amazon EMR to run each Spark process, send completion messages to an SQS queue, and invoke the third process with a Lambda function</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue workflows to run the first two AWS Glue Spark jobs in parallel, and start the third AWS Glue job after both jobs finish</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue workflows are designed to orchestrate multiple AWS Glue jobs, crawlers, and triggers as a single ETL pipeline. The workflow can run independent jobs in parallel and then use a conditional trigger to start a downstream job only after the required upstream jobs complete successfully.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue Spark jobs can process large datasets from Amazon S3 and write transformed output back to Amazon S3. This fits the requirement to convert 650 GB of CSV files to Apache Parquet and then run a correlation process after the first two processes complete.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational overhead because AWS Glue provides both the serverless Spark processing environment and the workflow orchestration layer. The company does not need to operate Airflow, manage EMR clusters, build SQS-based completion tracking, or package containerized Spark jobs.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Batch to run three containerized Spark jobs on managed compute environments, and use DynamoDB to track when the first two jobs finish</strong> - AWS Batch can run containerized batch jobs and can manage compute environments for large processing tasks. DynamoDB could track job completion state if the team builds that coordination logic.</p>\n\n<p>That design adds avoidable custom operations. The company would need to build and maintain container images, Spark dependencies, completion tracking, and job coordination logic, while AWS Glue provides managed Spark jobs and workflow orchestration for this use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Step Functions to orchestrate multiple Lambda functions, and configure the third Lambda function to run after the first two functions finish</strong> - Step Functions can coordinate serverless workflows and can start one task after other tasks finish. Lambda functions can process event-driven tasks and integrate with many AWS services.</p>\n\n<p>The issue with this approach is that Lambda is not a good fit for long-running, memory-intensive processing of hundreds of gigabytes of data. Lambda also does not provide the Spark-based distributed processing model that AWS Glue jobs provide for this ETL workload.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon EMR to run each Spark process, send completion messages to an SQS queue, and invoke the third process with a Lambda function</strong> - Amazon EMR can run Apache Spark jobs and can process large data volumes from Amazon S3. SQS and Lambda can help coordinate custom workflow signaling when one process must wait for others to complete.</p>\n\n<p>This creates additional operational overhead. The team must manage EMR cluster lifecycle, Spark runtime configuration, queue messages, completion tracking, and Lambda orchestration instead of using AWS Glue workflows and Glue Spark jobs directly.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/creating_running_workflows.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/creating_running_workflows.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/</a></p>"},{"id":21,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A company stores petabytes of data in hundreds of Amazon S3 buckets in the S3 Standard storage class. The data supports several analytics, visualization, and reporting-specific workflows. The data access patterns are unpredictable and variable. Some data is not accessed for months but needs to be available in milliseconds when accessed. The company wants to optimize the costs of storage.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution can address these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the default access tier</p>","<p>Opt for Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration for milliseconds latencies</p>","<p>Leverage S3 Storage Lens advanced metrics to determine when to move objects to more cost-optimized storage classes</p>","<p>Use Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Instant Access tier</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the default access tier</strong></p>\n\n<p>The Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class automatically stores objects in three access tiers. One tier is optimized for frequent access, one lower-cost tier is optimized for infrequent access, and another very low-cost tier is optimized for rarely accessed data. For a low monthly object monitoring and automation charge, S3 Intelligent-Tiering monitors access patterns and automatically moves objects to the Infrequent Access tier when they haven't been accessed for 30 consecutive days. After 90 days of no access, the objects are moved to the Archive Instant Access tier without performance impact or operational overhead.</p>\n\n<p>S3 Intelligent-Tiering is the recommended storage class for data with unknown, changing, or unpredictable access patterns, independent of object size or retention period, such as data lakes, data analytics, and new applications.</p>\n\n<p>S3 Intelligent-Tiering access tiers:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/7bc764f6ecf21602c8154258ff2a6002bf53cb10c25c965697b5e6bb86f38e30.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/7bc764f6ecf21602c8154258ff2a6002bf53cb10c25c965697b5e6bb86f38e30.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage S3 Storage Lens advanced metrics to determine when to move objects to more cost-optimized storage classes</strong> - Amazon S3 Storage Lens provides organization-wide visibility into object storage usage and activity trends, as well as actionable recommendations to optimize costs and apply data protection best practices. Storage Lens offers an interactive dashboard containing a single view of your object storage usage and activity across tens or hundreds of accounts in your organization, with drill-downs to generate insights at multiple aggregation levels.</p>\n\n<p>S3 Storage Lens contains more than 60 metrics, grouped into free metrics and advanced metrics (available for an additional cost). Within free metrics, you receive metrics to analyze usage (based on a daily snapshot of your objects), which are organized into the categories of cost optimization, data protection, access management, performance, and events. Within advanced metrics, you receive metrics related to the activity (such as request counts), deeper cost optimization (such as S3 Lifecycle rule counts), additional data protection (such as S3 Replication rule counts), and detailed status codes (such as 403 authorization errors).</p>\n\n<p><em>Opt for Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration for milliseconds latencies</em>* - Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration can speed up content transfers to and from Amazon S3 by as much as 50-500% for long-distance transfer of larger objects. Customers who have either web or mobile applications with widespread users or applications hosted far away from their S3 bucket can experience long and variable upload and download speeds over the Internet. This option acts as a distractor since S3 Transfer Acceleration will not reduce the cost of storage on Amazon S3, which is the main requirement for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Instant Access tier</strong> - If an object is not accessed for 90 consecutive days, the object moves to the Archive Instant Access tier of Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering. The Archive Instant Access tier provides low latency and high-throughput performance. This is an automatic tier and one cannot opt for this tier manually.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/faqs/</a></p>"},{"id":22,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A multi-national retail company uses separate AWS accounts for their business units. The finance team has an encrypted snapshot of an Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) instance that uses the default AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key. The finance team wants to share the encrypted snapshot with their Audit team that uses another AWS account.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following solutions would you recommend to address the given use-case?</p>","answers":["<p>Add the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account</p>","<p>Add the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account</p>","<p>Add the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account</p>","<p>Add the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Add the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account</strong> - You can't share a snapshot that's encrypted using the default AWS KMS encryption key.</p>\n\n<p>To share an encrypted Amazon RDS DB snapshot:</p>\n\n<p>Add the target account to a custom (non-default) KMS key.</p>\n\n<p>Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account.</p>\n\n<p>Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account.</p>\n\n<p>Detailed steps to implement this solution:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/5be6f102a9b0024228d348f9eed94d16b7650bed0816e7deae14f73d5dbda93f.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/5be6f102a9b0024228d348f9eed94d16b7650bed0816e7deae14f73d5dbda93f.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/share-encrypted-rds-snapshot-kms-key/\">https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/share-encrypted-rds-snapshot-kms-key/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p>\"Add the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\"</p>\n\n<p>\"Add the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\"</p>\n\n<p>\"Add the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\"</p>\n\n<p>These three options contradict the explanation provided above, so these are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/share-encrypted-rds-snapshot-kms-key/\">https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/share-encrypted-rds-snapshot-kms-key/</a></p>"},{"id":23,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A travel booking company needs to store semi-structured reservation session data for a customer-facing application. The application writes session records only when customers create or modify itineraries, but it reads the records frequently during search, checkout, and support workflows. The database layer must be serverless, require minimal administration, and return frequently accessed records within milliseconds.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Store the reservation session data in an Amazon RDS for MySQL cluster and configure RDS Optimized Reads for the database instances</p>","<p>Store the reservation session data in an Amazon S3 Apache Iceberg table and enable S3 Transfer Acceleration for low-latency object access</p>","<p>Store the reservation session data in an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster, configure the application to write all transactional records directly to the cache, and create daily snapshots for durability</p>","<p>Store the reservation session data in an Amazon DynamoDB table and configure DynamoDB Accelerator to cache frequently accessed items</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the reservation session data in an Amazon DynamoDB table and configure DynamoDB Accelerator to cache frequently accessed items</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon DynamoDB is a serverless, fully managed NoSQL database that supports key-value and document data models. This makes it a good fit for semi-structured transactional data such as reservation session objects, customer selections, and itinerary metadata.</p>\n\n<p>DynamoDB provides single-digit millisecond performance at scale without requiring the company to manage database servers, storage allocation, patching, or cluster capacity in the same way as traditional databases. The application writes infrequently but reads frequently, which aligns well with DynamoDB access patterns when the table design uses appropriate partition keys and access patterns.</p>\n\n<p>DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) improves read performance for read-heavy workloads by adding a fully managed in-memory cache in front of DynamoDB. Because the application frequently retrieves the same session data and requires millisecond retrieval, DAX can reduce read latency and offload repeated reads from the DynamoDB table.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the reservation session data in an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster, configure the application to write all transactional records directly to the cache, and create daily snapshots for durability</strong> - Amazon ElastiCache for Redis can provide very low-latency access for cached data. However, using Redis as the primary transactional data store for all session records increases durability, recovery, and data modeling risk compared with DynamoDB.</p>\n\n<p>This option also requires the company to manage cache-specific design considerations such as memory sizing, eviction behavior, cluster configuration, and snapshot recovery. DynamoDB with DAX provides a more appropriate primary database plus cache pattern with lower operational overhead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the reservation session data in an Amazon RDS for MySQL cluster and configure RDS Optimized Reads for the database instances</strong> - Amazon RDS for MySQL can store transactional data in a relational database. However, an RDS cluster requires more database administration than a serverless NoSQL database for this use case.</p>\n\n<p>RDS Optimized Reads can improve query processing that uses temporary objects on supported instance classes. It does not provide a serverless document database model or an in-memory acceleration layer for frequently accessed semi-structured records.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the reservation session data in an Amazon S3 Apache Iceberg table and enable S3 Transfer Acceleration for low-latency object access</strong> - Apache Iceberg tables in Amazon S3 are designed for analytical table workloads, especially data lake queries, schema evolution, and large-scale analytics. They are not designed for millisecond transactional reads from a customer-facing application.</p>\n\n<p>S3 Transfer Acceleration does not solve the database access pattern. It improves data transfer into or out of S3 over long distances, but it does not provide low-latency item retrieval, transactional key-value lookups, or caching for frequent application reads.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/evaluate-dax-suitability.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/evaluate-dax-suitability.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless/latest/devguide/starter-dynamodb.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless/latest/devguide/starter-dynamodb.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/reduce-latency-and-cost-in-read-heavy-applications-using-amazon-dynamodb-accelerator/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/reduce-latency-and-cost-in-read-heavy-applications-using-amazon-dynamodb-accelerator/</a></p>"},{"id":24,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A healthcare technology company stores protected analytics files in an Amazon S3 bucket in the eu-central-1 Region. The company must restrict access to the S3 objects and encrypt the objects by using AWS managed keys. A serverless application uses an AWS Lambda function to connect to a reporting database, and the company must store and rotate the database credentials automatically.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-KMS with the AWS managed key for Amazon S3, and store the database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation by a Lambda rotation function</p>","<p>Use S3 bucket ACLs to restrict access to the analytics files, configure SSE-KMS encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with rotation performed by a scheduled Lambda function</p>","<p>Use Amazon CloudFront signed URLs to restrict access to the S3 objects, configure SSE-KMS with a customer managed KMS key, store database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager, and require the application team to rotate the secret manually after each maintenance window</p>","<p>Use IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-S3 encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with a scheduled Lambda function that rotates the credentials</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-KMS with the AWS managed key for Amazon S3, and store the database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation by a Lambda rotation function</strong></p>\n\n<p>IAM policies can restrict which principals can access Amazon S3 buckets and objects. The company can grant the Lambda function and authorized users only the required S3 actions on the required bucket or object prefixes, which supports least privilege access to the protected analytics files.</p>\n\n<p>SSE-KMS can encrypt Amazon S3 objects by using AWS KMS keys. When Amazon S3 uses SSE-KMS and the request or bucket configuration does not specify a KMS key ID, Amazon S3 uses the AWS managed key for Amazon S3, which is <code>aws/s3</code>. This satisfies the requirement to encrypt the data by using AWS managed keys while still using AWS KMS integration.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Secrets Manager is the correct service for storing and automatically rotating database credentials. Secrets Manager can invoke a Lambda rotation function according to a configured rotation schedule. The application Lambda function can retrieve the current credential from Secrets Manager at runtime instead of storing static credentials in configuration.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use S3 bucket ACLs to restrict access to the analytics files, configure SSE-KMS encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with rotation performed by a scheduled Lambda function</strong> - S3 ACLs are a legacy access control mechanism and are not the preferred way to manage access for modern S3 security designs. AWS recommends using IAM policies and bucket policies for most access control requirements.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store can store secure string values, but it does not provide the same managed database credential rotation workflow as Secrets Manager. A custom scheduled Lambda rotation process adds operational work and is less suitable than Secrets Manager automatic rotation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon CloudFront signed URLs to restrict access to the S3 objects, configure SSE-KMS with a customer managed KMS key, store database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager, and require the application team to rotate the secret manually after each maintenance window</strong> - CloudFront signed URLs can restrict access to content delivered through CloudFront. However, this requirement concerns S3 data access controls and database credentials for a Lambda function, not content distribution through CloudFront.</p>\n\n<p>This option also uses a customer managed KMS key instead of an AWS managed key and requires manual secret rotation. Manual rotation does not meet the requirement to rotate the database credentials automatically.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-S3 encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with a scheduled Lambda function that rotates the credentials</strong> - IAM policies can restrict access to Amazon S3 data, and SSE-S3 provides server-side encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys. However, this option stores credentials in Systems Manager Parameter Store and uses a scheduled Lambda function to rotate them.</p>\n\n<p>That rotation model requires the company to build, test, monitor, and maintain custom rotation logic. Secrets Manager provides a purpose-built managed workflow for automatic secret rotation with Lambda rotation functions.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security-iam.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security-iam.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/specifying-kms-encryption.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/specifying-kms-encryption.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-encryption.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-encryption.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_turn-on-for-db.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_turn-on-for-db.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/rotate-amazon-rds-database-credentials-automatically-with-aws-secrets-manager/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/rotate-amazon-rds-database-credentials-automatically-with-aws-secrets-manager/</a></p>"},{"id":25,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A data engineer is supporting an AWS Glue workflow that validates and transforms daily claims data. The workflow sometimes fails when the input files contain data quality issues. A business operations team needs to receive an email notification whenever an AWS Glue job in the workflow fails in the future.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Create an SNS FIFO topic, subscribe the team's email address to the topic, and create a Lambda function that publishes messages when AWS Glue job state changes to FAILED</p>","<p>Create an SQS standard queue, subscribe the team's email address to the queue, and create an EventBridge rule that sends failed AWS Glue job events to the queue</p>","<p>Create an SNS standard topic, subscribe the team's email address to the topic, and create an EventBridge rule that sends AWS Glue FAILED job state change events to the topic</p>","<p>Create a CloudWatch alarm on AWS Glue job failure metrics, configure the alarm to write notifications to Amazon S3, and ask the business team to review the S3 objects</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an SNS standard topic, subscribe the team's email address to the topic, and create an EventBridge rule that sends AWS Glue FAILED job state change events to the topic</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon EventBridge can receive AWS Glue job state change events. The data engineer can create an EventBridge rule that matches events from AWS Glue where the job state is FAILED.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon SNS standard topics support email subscriptions. After the business operations team confirms the email subscription, SNS can deliver notifications when EventBridge sends matching failed job events to the topic.</p>\n\n<p>This solution meets the requirement because it uses managed event routing and managed email notification delivery. The company does not need to write custom polling code, manually inspect job logs, or build a separate workflow just to notify users when an AWS Glue job fails.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/1990ad72c10ab161a5e89e4effd022b3fc3e002d04a920c276ad70b6bb20381a.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/1990ad72c10ab161a5e89e4effd022b3fc3e002d04a920c276ad70b6bb20381a.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an SQS standard queue, subscribe the team's email address to the queue, and create an EventBridge rule that sends failed AWS Glue job events to the queue</strong> - Amazon SQS standard queues can decouple producers and consumers and can store messages for asynchronous processing. EventBridge can also send events to supported targets such as SQS queues.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because SQS does not directly subscribe email addresses and send email notifications. The company would need another consumer, such as Lambda, to read queue messages and send email, which adds unnecessary operational work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an SNS FIFO topic, subscribe the team's email address to the topic, and create a Lambda function that publishes messages when AWS Glue job state changes to FAILED</strong> - A Lambda function could inspect AWS Glue job state changes and publish a notification message. SNS FIFO topics are useful when strict message ordering and deduplication are required for application-to-application messaging.</p>\n\n<p>The issue with this approach is that SNS FIFO topics do not support direct email delivery to customer-managed endpoints. A standard SNS topic is the appropriate topic type for email notifications.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a CloudWatch alarm on AWS Glue job failure metrics, configure the alarm to write notifications to Amazon S3, and ask the business team to review the S3 objects</strong> - CloudWatch alarms can notify teams when metrics cross configured thresholds. They are useful for operational monitoring and alerting.</p>\n\n<p>This design sends information to Amazon S3 instead of emailing the business team. It also requires users to manually review files, while the requirement asks for an email notification whenever the workflow fails.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/automating-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-events.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/automating-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-events.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/ref/events-ref-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/ref/events-ref-glue.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/fifo-message-delivery.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/fifo-message-delivery.html</a></p>"},{"id":26,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A video production company uploads large raw footage files to an Amazon S3 bucket in the us-west-2 Region for transcoding and quality checks. After the transcoding workflow finishes, the company must retain the original raw files for 75 days in case editors need to reprocess any footage. After 75 days, the company no longer needs the originals and wants to delete them to reduce storage costs. The processed videos are stored in a separate S3 bucket.</p>\n\n<p>Which S3 Lifecycle configuration will meet these requirements for the original files MOST cost-effectively?</p>","answers":["<p>Store the original files in S3 Standard-Infrequent Access immediately after upload, keep the files for 75 days, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files when the retention period ends</p>","<p>Store the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days, transition the files to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and then configure expiration after archival storage</p>","<p>Store the original files in S3 Intelligent-Tiering for 75 days, enable bucket versioning, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files after the retention period</p>","<p>Store the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the current versions of the files after the retention period</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the current versions of the files after the retention period</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 Lifecycle can expire current object versions after a specified number of days from object creation. The company can create a Lifecycle rule for the raw footage bucket or for a specific prefix that stores original files and set the expiration action to 75 days.</p>\n\n<p>This solution keeps the original files available in S3 Standard throughout the reprocessing window. S3 Standard is appropriate when the company might need immediate access to the originals during the first 75 days after upload.</p>\n\n<p>This option is the most cost-effective fit because the company does not need the original files after the retention period. Expiring the files directly avoids unnecessary transition requests, minimum storage duration charges, version storage, Object Lock configuration, or long-term archival storage for files that the company plans to delete.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the original files in S3 Intelligent-Tiering for 75 days, enable bucket versioning, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files after the retention period</strong> - S3 Intelligent-Tiering can optimize storage costs when access patterns are unknown or change over time. However, it also has monitoring and automation considerations, and the company has a clear fixed retention period for the original files.</p>\n\n<p>Enabling versioning also can increase storage consumption because noncurrent versions may remain after updates or deletes unless the Lifecycle configuration also manages noncurrent versions. The requirement does not need version history for the raw originals.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the original files in S3 Standard-Infrequent Access immediately after upload, keep the files for 75 days, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files when the retention period ends</strong> - S3 Standard-Infrequent Access can reduce storage cost for infrequently accessed objects, but it has a minimum storage duration of 30 days and retrieval charges. It is best when objects remain stored long enough and are accessed infrequently.</p>\n\n<p>This option transitions the files immediately even though editors might need to reprocess them during the retention period. For a short, fixed retention window with possible immediate reprocessing needs, direct expiration from S3 Standard is the simpler and safer choice.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days, transition the files to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and then configure expiration after archival storage</strong> - S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is designed for archive data that does not require immediate access. It also has a minimum storage duration of 90 days, so transitioning the objects around the end of a 75-day retention window can introduce unnecessary minimum-duration charges.</p>\n\n<p>The company does not need long-term storage after the retention period. It should expire the original files directly instead of moving them to an archive class before deletion.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/how-to-set-lifecycle-configuration-intro.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/how-to-set-lifecycle-configuration-intro.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intro-lifecycle-rules.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intro-lifecycle-rules.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/glacier-storage-classes.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/glacier-storage-classes.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/expiring-amazon-s3-objects-based-on-last-accessed-date-to-decrease-costs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/expiring-amazon-s3-objects-based-on-last-accessed-date-to-decrease-costs/</a></p>"},{"id":27,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A healthcare analytics company stores sensitive claims-processing files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The files support downstream compliance investigations and must be protected from accidental deletion by application users and administrators. A data engineer must implement controls that help preserve deleted objects and add stronger protection against permanent deletion.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Configure AWS Config remediation actions to automatically restore objects after users delete them from the S3 bucket</p>","<p>Enable versioning on the S3 bucket and configure MFA Delete for the bucket</p>","<p>Enable S3 Replication to copy objects to another bucket and leverage the replica bucket to prevent accidental deletions in the source bucket</p>","<p>Configure an S3 bucket policy that denies requests that create delete markers for objects in the bucket</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable versioning on the S3 bucket and configure MFA Delete for the bucket</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 Versioning helps protect objects from accidental deletion. When a user sends a simple delete request to a versioning-enabled bucket, Amazon S3 does not permanently delete the object. Instead, Amazon S3 inserts a delete marker and preserves the previous object version.</p>\n\n<p>MFA Delete adds stronger protection for destructive actions. When MFA Delete is enabled, the bucket owner must provide MFA authentication to permanently delete an object version or change the bucket versioning state. This adds friction and helps prevent accidental or unauthorized permanent deletion.</p>\n\n<p>This solution directly meets the requirement because it combines recoverability with additional protection against permanent removal. Users can recover previous versions after accidental deletion, and MFA Delete reduces the risk that someone permanently deletes versions without the required MFA validation.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/04f1b4d2ce3d330225e0c8aa3c9f5d33d901ae84ac052fe78f59cbbe98b2d7e1.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/04f1b4d2ce3d330225e0c8aa3c9f5d33d901ae84ac052fe78f59cbbe98b2d7e1.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/zero-downtime-amazon-s3-versioning-architectural-patterns-for-mission-critical-workloads/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/zero-downtime-amazon-s3-versioning-architectural-patterns-for-mission-critical-workloads/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure an S3 bucket policy that denies requests that create delete markers for objects in the bucket</strong> - A bucket policy can deny delete actions, but the option focuses on denying the creation of delete markers. Delete markers are created by Amazon S3 when a simple delete request occurs in a versioning-enabled or versioning-suspended bucket.</p>\n\n<p>This option does not provide the correct native mechanism to preserve objects from accidental deletion. Versioning and MFA Delete provide purpose-built protection, while denying delete marker creation is not the proper control pattern.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable S3 Replication to copy objects to another bucket and leverage the replica bucket to prevent accidental deletions in the source bucket</strong> - S3 Replication can copy objects to another bucket for durability, cross-Region resilience, or account separation. A replica bucket can help with recovery strategies when configured carefully.</p>\n\n<p>However, replication alone does not prevent accidental deletion in the source bucket. It also does not provide the same direct protection against simple deletes and permanent version deletion that S3 Versioning with MFA Delete provides.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure AWS Config remediation actions to automatically restore objects after users delete them from the S3 bucket</strong> - AWS Config can evaluate resource configurations and can invoke remediation actions when resources drift from desired configuration. This is useful for detecting and correcting noncompliant bucket settings.</p>\n\n<p>However, AWS Config remediation is not the best mechanism to prevent users from deleting S3 objects. It does not provide object-version preservation or MFA-based protection for permanent object version deletion.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/DeletingObjects.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/DeletingObjects.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/MultiFactorAuthenticationDelete.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/MultiFactorAuthenticationDelete.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/UsingMFADelete.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/UsingMFADelete.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/versioning-workflows.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/versioning-workflows.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/zero-downtime-amazon-s3-versioning-architectural-patterns-for-mission-critical-workloads/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/zero-downtime-amazon-s3-versioning-architectural-patterns-for-mission-critical-workloads/</a></p>"},{"id":28,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A company uploads all of its data to an Amazon S3 bucket. While multiple formats of data can be uploaded to the bucket, the downstream applications need to consume data only the .csv files are uploaded. These .csv files have to be transformed into Apache Parquet format for downstream consumption.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following options meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Set up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as <code>s3:*</code>. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the destination as Amazon EventBridge and trigger the Lambda function from EventBridge</p>","<p>Set up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code>. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification</p>","<p>Set up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code>. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the destination as Amazon EventBridge and trigger the Lambda function from EventBridge</p>","<p>Set up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as <code>s3:*</code>. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code>. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification</strong></p>\n\n<p>s3:ObjectCreated:* - This event type will publish the following events : s3:ObjectCreated:Put, s3:ObjectCreated:Post, s3:ObjectCreated:Copy, s3:ObjectCreated:CompleteMultipartUpload.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 API operations such as PUT, POST, and COPY can create an object. With these event types, you can enable notifications when an object is created using a specific API operation. Alternatively, you can use the s3:ObjectCreated:* event type to request notification regardless of the API that was used to create an object.</p>\n\n<p>s3:ObjectCreated:CompleteMultipartUpload includes objects that are created using UploadPartCopy for Copy operations.</p>\n\n<p>This is the correct option since we want to process the data only when .csv files are uploaded.</p>\n\n<p>You can configure notifications to be filtered by the prefix and suffix of the key name of objects. For example, you can set up a configuration where you're sent a notification only when image files with a \".jpg\" file name extension are added to a bucket.</p>\n\n<p>Example notification configuration for multiple non-overlapping suffixes:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/aab9a324cc2f7f4d99c49cb064ac94c2b75b5b96f7eb6a1a45a3054f3d19dcbb.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/aab9a324cc2f7f4d99c49cb064ac94c2b75b5b96f7eb6a1a45a3054f3d19dcbb.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as <code>s3:*</code>. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as <code>s3:*</code>. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification</strong></p>\n\n<p>Both these options have the event type as <code>s3:*</code>, which means ALL S3 event types will trigger a notification for downstream processing, which is unnecessary. The given use case states that you need to process only when .csv files are uploaded (that is, you need to set up processing only for <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code> type of events that have a suffix of .csv). So, both these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code>. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the destination as Amazon EventBridge and trigger the Lambda function from EventBridge</strong> - Unlike other destinations, you can either enable or disable events to be delivered to EventBridge for a bucket. If you enable delivery, all events are sent to EventBridge.</p>\n\n<p>Using Eventbridge to process S3 event notifications turns out to be a roundabout way of doing things for the given use case. There is no need to invoke the Lambda function via Eventbridge when you can configure Amazon S3 to directly trigger the Lambda function using the S3 event notifications. So, this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-event-types-and-destinations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-event-types-and-destinations.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-use-amazon-s3-event-notifications-with-amazon-eventbridge/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-use-amazon-s3-event-notifications-with-amazon-eventbridge/</a></p>"},{"id":29,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>An IT company is using AWS DMS to migrate its Amazon RDS for Oracle DB instance configured in a VPC in the us-east-1 Region to another VPC in the us-west-1 Region.</p>\n\n<p>Where would you place the DMS replication instance for the MOST optimal performance?</p>","answers":["<p>Create the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the target DB instance</p>","<p>Create the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the target DB instance</p>","<p>Create the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the source DB instance</p>","<p>Create the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the source DB instance</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the target DB instance</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) is a cloud service that makes it easy to migrate relational databases, data warehouses, NoSQL databases, and other types of data stores. AWS DMS is a server in the AWS Cloud that runs replication software. You create a source and target connection to tell AWS DMS where to extract from and load to. Then you schedule a task that runs on this server to move your data. AWS DMS creates the tables and associated primary keys if they don't exist on the target.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/dc969e3126391bce2679400ccfa3e1dfd5bc655d127cf726280ac066074076c5.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/dc969e3126391bce2679400ccfa3e1dfd5bc655d127cf726280ac066074076c5.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>While migrating an Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for Oracle source database to a different AWS Region, AWS suggests that you create the replication instance in the VPC of the target AWS Region. This is sufficient to get your solution working. However, to further optimize your solution, you should consider creating your replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the target DB instance.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6187dcbefd6e035edafc7350ec223be22e5fba7ec52eb2be9289e4ddcb985499.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/6187dcbefd6e035edafc7350ec223be22e5fba7ec52eb2be9289e4ddcb985499.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-amazon-rds-for-oracle-database-to-another-aws-account-and-aws-region-using-aws-dms-for-ongoing-replication.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-amazon-rds-for-oracle-database-to-another-aws-account-and-aws-region-using-aws-dms-for-ongoing-replication.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the source DB instance</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Create the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the target DB instance</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Create the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the source DB instance</strong></p>\n\n<p>These three options contradict the details mentioned in the explanation above, hence these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-amazon-rds-for-oracle-database-to-another-aws-account-and-aws-region-using-aws-dms-for-ongoing-replication.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-amazon-rds-for-oracle-database-to-another-aws-account-and-aws-region-using-aws-dms-for-ongoing-replication.html</a></p>"},{"id":30,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A retail operations company needs to create a one-time executive performance report by joining data from Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3. The company wants to avoid building a temporary consolidated data store or moving large volumes of data between services. The reporting team also wants to minimize the time required to run the analysis.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon Athena Federated Query with Athena data source connectors to join the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3 datasets in place for the one-time report</p>","<p>Build an AWS Glue ETL pipeline to extract data from all sources, centralize the data in Amazon S3, and run Amazon Athena queries against the consolidated dataset</p>","<p>Create AWS Glue crawlers for each source, export the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, and Amazon Redshift datasets to Amazon S3, convert every exported dataset to Apache Parquet, catalog the converted files, and run the final join by using Amazon Athena</p>","<p>Capture DynamoDB changes with DynamoDB Streams, migrate Aurora PostgreSQL data with AWS DMS, export Amazon Redshift data to Amazon S3, and query the combined files with Redshift Spectrum</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena Federated Query with Athena data source connectors to join the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3 datasets in place for the one-time report</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Athena Federated Query allows Athena to run SQL queries across data stored in sources other than Amazon S3 by using data source connectors. Athena supports connectors for sources such as Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Redshift, and JDBC-compatible relational databases, while Athena can also query data stored in Amazon S3 directly.</p>\n\n<p>This solution meets the one-time reporting requirement because the company can query the data in place instead of building an ETL pipeline or creating a temporary consolidated copy of every dataset. Avoiding unnecessary data movement reduces setup effort and helps the reporting team produce the analysis more quickly.</p>\n\n<p>Athena Federated Query is also suitable when teams need to join data across multiple systems for ad hoc analysis. The company can define the required connectors, run the federated SQL query, and produce the report without operating an EMR cluster, managing a large migration workflow, or duplicating data into a new analytics store.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c81456d768bcee1e5b87982f99e811f5e1ba143e71ea532454107d06d24e782f.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/c81456d768bcee1e5b87982f99e811f5e1ba143e71ea532454107d06d24e782f.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/82b9940d6c775343e6c8ea63d1d1de1fa9b80330ce6af2bba0c0b8987b3fd0ed.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/82b9940d6c775343e6c8ea63d1d1de1fa9b80330ce6af2bba0c0b8987b3fd0ed.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Build an AWS Glue ETL pipeline to extract data from all sources, centralize the data in Amazon S3, and run Amazon Athena queries against the consolidated dataset</strong> - AWS Glue can extract, transform, and load data from multiple sources into Amazon S3. Amazon Athena can then query the centralized dataset after the ETL pipeline finishes.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option moves and centralizes data even though the company needs only a one-time report. Building and running the ETL pipeline increases setup time and creates unnecessary data movement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create AWS Glue crawlers for each source, export the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, and Amazon Redshift datasets to Amazon S3, convert every exported dataset to Apache Parquet, catalog the converted files, and run the final join by using Amazon Athena</strong> - AWS Glue crawlers can catalog datasets, and converting files to Apache Parquet can improve query performance for repeated analytical workloads. Athena can then query the optimized data in Amazon S3.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option exports and converts every dataset before the report can run. The approach is wordier, slower to prepare, and more operationally heavy than querying the sources in place with Athena Federated Query.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Capture DynamoDB changes with DynamoDB Streams, migrate Aurora PostgreSQL data with AWS DMS, export Amazon Redshift data to Amazon S3, and query the combined files with Redshift Spectrum</strong> - DynamoDB Streams, AWS DMS, Amazon Redshift exports, and Redshift Spectrum can support data movement and data lake query patterns. This design could eventually create a consolidated analytical dataset in Amazon S3.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option is unnecessarily complex for a one-time report. It requires several movement mechanisms and does not minimize query preparation time compared with Athena Federated Query.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/federated-queries.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/federated-queries.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connectors-dynamodb.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connectors-dynamodb.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connectors-redshift.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connectors-redshift.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/improve-federated-queries-with-predicate-pushdown-in-amazon-athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/improve-federated-queries-with-predicate-pushdown-in-amazon-athena/</a></p>"},{"id":31,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>An application hosted on Amazon EC2 contains sensitive personal information about all its customers and needs to be protected from all types of cyber-attacks. The company is considering using the AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) to handle this requirement.</p>\n\n<p>Can you identify the correct solution leveraging the capabilities of AWS WAF?</p>","answers":["<p>Configure an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to balance the workload for all the Amazon EC2 instances. Configure Amazon CloudFront to distribute from an Application Load Balancer since AWS WAF cannot be directly configured on ALB. This configuration not only provides necessary safety but is scalable too</p>","<p>Create Amazon CloudFront distribution for the application on Amazon EC2 instances. Deploy AWS WAF on Amazon CloudFront to provide the necessary safety measures</p>","<p>AWS WAF can be directly configured on Amazon EC2 instances for ensuring the security of the underlying application data</p>","<p>AWS WAF can be directly configured only on an Application Load Balancer or an Amazon API Gateway. One of these two services can then be configured with Amazon EC2 to build the needed secure architecture</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create Amazon CloudFront distribution for the application on Amazon EC2 instances. Deploy AWS WAF on Amazon CloudFront to provide the necessary safety measures</strong></p>\n\n<p>When you use AWS WAF with Amazon CloudFront, you can protect your applications running on any HTTP web server, whether it's a webserver that's running in Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) or a web server that you manage privately. You can also configure Amazon CloudFront to require HTTPS between CloudFront and your own web server, as well as between viewers and Amazon CloudFront.</p>\n\n<p>AWS WAF is tightly integrated with Amazon CloudFront and the Application Load Balancer (ALB), services that AWS customers commonly use to deliver content for their websites and applications. When you use AWS WAF on Amazon CloudFront, your rules run in all AWS Edge Locations, located around the world close to your end-users. This means security doesn’t come at the expense of performance. Blocked requests are stopped before they reach your web servers. When you use AWS WAF on Application Load Balancer, your rules run in the region and can be used to protect internet-facing as well as internal load balancers.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to balance the workload for all the Amazon EC2 instances. Configure Amazon CloudFront to distribute from an Application Load Balancer since AWS WAF cannot be directly configured on ALB. This configuration not only provides necessary safety but is scalable too</strong> - This statement is wrong. You can configure AWS WAF on Application Load Balancers (ALB).</p>\n\n<p><strong>AWS WAF can be directly configured on Amazon EC2 instances for ensuring the security of the underlying application data</strong> - AWS WAF can be deployed on Amazon CloudFront, the Application Load Balancer (ALB), and Amazon API Gateway. It cannot be configured directly on an Amazon EC2 instance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>AWS WAF can be directly configured only on an Application Load Balancer or an Amazon API Gateway. One of these two services can then be configured with Amazon EC2 to build the needed secure architecture</strong> - This statement is only partially correct. AWS WAF can also be deployed on Amazon CloudFront service.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/waf/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/waf/faqs/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/cloudfront-features.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/cloudfront-features.html</a></p>"},{"id":32,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A leading video streaming service delivers billions of hours of content from Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) to customers around the world. Amazon S3 also serves as the data lake for its big data analytics solution. The data lake has a staging zone where intermediary query results are kept only for 24 hours. These results are also heavily referenced by other parts of the analytics pipeline.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is the MOST cost-effective strategy for storing this intermediary query data?</p>","answers":["<p>Store the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval storage class</p>","<p>Store the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access storage class</p>","<p>Store the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access storage class</p>","<p>Store the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard storage class</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard storage class</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 Standard offers high durability, availability, and performance object storage for frequently accessed data. Because it delivers low latency and high throughput, S3 Standard is appropriate for a wide variety of use cases, including cloud applications, dynamic websites, content distribution, mobile and gaming applications, and big data analytics.\nAs there is no minimum storage duration charge and no retrieval fee (remember that intermediary query results are heavily referenced by other parts of the analytics pipeline), this is the MOST cost-effective storage class amongst the given options.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval storage class</strong> - Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval delivers the fastest access to archive storage, with the same throughput and milliseconds access as the S3 Standard and S3 Standard-IA storage classes. S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval is ideal for archive data that needs immediate access, such as medical images, news media assets, or user-generated content archives.</p>\n\n<p>The minimum storage duration charge is 90 days, so this option is NOT cost-effective because intermediary query results need to be kept only for 24 hours. Hence this option is not correct.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access storage class</strong> - Amazon S3 Standard-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. S3 Standard-IA offers high durability, high throughput, and low latency of S3 Standard, with a low per GB storage price and per GB retrieval fee. This combination of low cost and high performance makes S3 Standard-IA ideal for long-term storage, backups, and as a data store for disaster recovery files.\nThe minimum storage duration charge is 30 days, so this option is NOT cost-effective because intermediary query results need to be kept only for 24 hours. Hence this option is not correct.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access storage class</strong> - Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Unlike other S3 Storage Classes which store data in a minimum of three Availability Zones (AZs), S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single AZ and costs 20% less than S3 Standard-IA.\nThe minimum storage duration charge is 30 days, so this option is NOT cost-effective because intermediary query results need to be kept only for 24 hours. Hence this option is not correct.</p>\n\n<p>To summarize again, S3 Standard-IA and S3 One Zone-IA have a minimum storage duration charge of 30 days (so instead of 24 hours, you end up paying for 30 days). S3 Standard-IA and S3 One Zone-IA also have retrieval charges (as the results are heavily referenced by other parts of the analytics pipeline, so the retrieval costs would be pretty high). Therefore, these storage classes are not cost-optimal for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a></p>"},{"id":33,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A health care application processes the real-time health data of the patients into an analytics workflow. With a sharp increase in the number of users, the system has become slow and sometimes even unresponsive as it does not have a retry mechanism. The startup is looking at a scalable solution that has minimal implementation overhead.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following would you recommend as a scalable alternative to the current solution?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing</p>","<p>Use Amazon API Gateway with the existing REST-based interface to create a high-performing architecture</p>","<p>Use Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing</p>","<p>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the data, process it using AWS Lambda, or run analytics using Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the data, process it using AWS Lambda, or run analytics using Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) is a massively scalable and durable real-time data streaming service with support for a retry mechanism. KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events. The data collected is available in milliseconds to enable real-time analytics use cases such as real-time dashboards, real-time anomaly detection, dynamic pricing, and more.</p>\n\n<p>KDS makes sure your streaming data is available to multiple real-time analytics applications, to Amazon S3, or AWS Lambda within 70 milliseconds of the data being collected. Amazon Kinesis data streams scale from megabytes to terabytes per hour and scale from thousands to millions of PUT records per second. You can dynamically adjust the throughput of your stream at any time based on the volume of your input data.</p>\n\n<p>How Data Streams Work:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/165666b0785d6f6c80178f86b2f78e5004b43597eaec81582bcd6b93aece8c73.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/165666b0785d6f6c80178f86b2f78e5004b43597eaec81582bcd6b93aece8c73.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/?nc=sn&amp;loc=2&amp;dn=2\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/?nc=sn&amp;loc=2&amp;dn=2</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing</strong> - Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) is a fully managed messaging service for both application-to-application (A2A) and application-to-person (A2P) communication. Amazon SNS is a push mechanism that does not support robust retry mechanisms, as is needed in the current use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing</strong> - Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) is a messaging service that helps in decoupling systems and reducing the complexity of architecture. Amazon SQS can still work but Amazon Kinesis Data streams is custom-made for streaming real-time data.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon API Gateway with the existing REST-based interface to create a high-performing architecture</strong> - Amazon API Gateway is not meant for processing real-time streaming data.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/?nc=sn&amp;loc=2&amp;dn=2\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/?nc=sn&amp;loc=2&amp;dn=2</a></p>"},{"id":34,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A retail analytics company runs several concurrent reporting queries on Amazon Redshift sales and inventory tables throughout the day. Business users require consistent reporting snapshots four times each day. The company also runs ETL jobs that update product, customer, and store dimension tables while analysts run dashboard and ad hoc queries.</p>\n\n<p>The company has found that concurrent reporting workloads create lock contention during ETL updates. During peak business hours, dashboards experience query timeouts and some ETL transactions encounter deadlocks. The company needs to isolate the reporting workload from the ETL workload while maintaining consistent access to current data.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will fix this issue?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum for all analytical queries against data in Amazon S3, and continue running ETL updates on the Amazon Redshift dimension tables with transaction isolation</p>","<p>Deploy separate Amazon Redshift clusters for ETL and analytics workloads, use Amazon Redshift data sharing to expose live data to the analytics cluster, and use cross-database queries when users need consistent access across shared databases</p>","<p>Increase the workload management concurrency slots on the existing Amazon Redshift cluster, assign ETL queries and reporting queries to separate queues, and keep both workloads running against the same updated dimension tables</p>","<p>Configure Amazon Redshift federated queries to access operational source databases directly, create read replicas for the source systems, and route analytical queries to the read replicas instead of Redshift tables</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Deploy separate Amazon Redshift clusters for ETL and analytics workloads, use Amazon Redshift data sharing to expose live data to the analytics cluster, and use cross-database queries when users need consistent access across shared databases</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift data sharing allows one Redshift cluster or workgroup to share live data with another Redshift cluster or workgroup without copying or moving the data. The ETL cluster can act as the producer, and the analytics cluster can act as the consumer that serves dashboard and ad hoc query workloads.</p>\n\n<p>This design isolates compute resources for ETL and analytics. ETL operations can update dimension tables on the producer cluster, while analysts run reporting workloads on a separate consumer cluster. This reduces lock contention, query timeouts, and deadlock risk that occur when both workload types compete on the same cluster.</p>\n\n<p>The solution also supports consistent access to current shared data. Redshift data sharing provides access to live data, and users can query shared databases from the consumer environment. Cross-database queries can help users access data across databases when the reporting model requires that pattern.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/1c98a57840908866e9daa6ff3e2b04d2dee40b1565eb56d8537fbb1db851723a.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/1c98a57840908866e9daa6ff3e2b04d2dee40b1565eb56d8537fbb1db851723a.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-data-sharing-best-practices-and-considerations/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-data-sharing-best-practices-and-considerations/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum for all analytical queries against data in Amazon S3, and continue running ETL updates on the Amazon Redshift dimension tables with transaction isolation</strong> - Amazon Redshift Spectrum allows Redshift to query external data stored in Amazon S3. This can be useful for data lake analytics, especially when teams need to query large external datasets without loading all data into Redshift.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option does not directly fix lock contention on the Redshift tables that ETL jobs update. The analytical workload still depends on a separate S3 data path, and the company would need additional processes to keep S3 data synchronized with Redshift tables.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Increase the workload management concurrency slots on the existing Amazon Redshift cluster, assign ETL queries and reporting queries to separate queues, and keep both workloads running against the same updated dimension tables</strong> - Workload management can help control query concurrency and allocate resources across queues. It can improve fairness and reduce resource pressure when workloads share a Redshift cluster.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option keeps ETL and reporting queries on the same cluster and the same updated tables. It may reduce resource contention, but it does not eliminate table-level lock contention or provide the stronger workload isolation that separate clusters with data sharing provide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure Amazon Redshift federated queries to access operational source databases directly, create read replicas for the source systems, and route analytical queries to the read replicas instead of Redshift tables</strong> - Federated queries let Amazon Redshift query data in external operational databases. Read replicas can help isolate read workloads for certain relational database sources.</p>\n\n<p>This option moves reporting access toward source systems rather than fixing contention in the Redshift analytics environment. It also does not maintain the Redshift-based curated dimension table model that the ETL process updates for reporting.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/datasharing.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/datasharing.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cross-database-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cross-database-overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-data-sharing-best-practices-and-considerations/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-data-sharing-best-practices-and-considerations/</a></p>"},{"id":35,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>An online marketplace needs to store and query catalog data for millions of products. Each product can have different attributes depending on the product type, such as size, warranty period, material, or supported device model. The application must retrieve product records by a primary identifier named SKU and must also support flexible access patterns by Department and Manufacturer. The workload has unpredictable traffic and must sustain sudden request spikes with single-digit millisecond latency.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon OpenSearch Serverless to store product documents, index the SKU and secondary attributes, and query all catalog records through search APIs</p>","<p>Use Amazon Aurora with a Multi-AZ deployment, add read replicas, and create indexes on SKU, Department, and Manufacturer for catalog queries</p>","<p>Use a DynamoDB table with on-demand capacity, store products by SKU as the primary key, and create global secondary indexes for Department and Manufacturer</p>","<p>Use Amazon Redshift Serverless to store product records in relational tables, distribute rows by SKU, and create materialized views for Department and Manufacturer access</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use a DynamoDB table with on-demand capacity, store products by SKU as the primary key, and create global secondary indexes for Department and Manufacturer</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon DynamoDB is a strong fit for product catalog data that has variable attributes. DynamoDB stores items in a flexible schema model, so each product item can include only the attributes that apply to that product type.</p>\n\n<p>The application must retrieve items by SKU and also support access by Department and Manufacturer. The table can use SKU as the primary key for direct item lookups. Global secondary indexes can support additional query patterns that use Department and Manufacturer as index keys.</p>\n\n<p>DynamoDB on-demand capacity is appropriate because the traffic is unpredictable and can spike suddenly. On-demand mode automatically adapts to request volume and supports single-digit millisecond performance without requiring the company to provision read and write capacity in advance.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0308ab5c28959092a3c1373d7c7e2cfe7fe020d772b90f7458e6a5f9fa3e7552.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/0308ab5c28959092a3c1373d7c7e2cfe7fe020d772b90f7458e6a5f9fa3e7552.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/35cb5b5dfe0f8df69831840a79ae128138d6095ff240ff5f8850cfbec7164b68.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/35cb5b5dfe0f8df69831840a79ae128138d6095ff240ff5f8850cfbec7164b68.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0a46939d68d7d6c1412f11818b1e8abf7bceb1e6723eb18df579ed8d58da6fd2.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/0a46939d68d7d6c1412f11818b1e8abf7bceb1e6723eb18df579ed8d58da6fd2.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Aurora with a Multi-AZ deployment, add read replicas, and create indexes on SKU, Department, and Manufacturer for catalog queries</strong> - Amazon Aurora can store product data in relational tables, and indexes can support lookups by SKU, Department, and Manufacturer. Multi-AZ deployments and read replicas can also improve availability and read scaling.</p>\n\n<p>However, the requirement calls for variable product attributes and unpredictable high-volume access with single-digit millisecond latency during traffic spikes. DynamoDB on-demand capacity is a better fit for a flexible, key-based operational workload that needs automatic scaling.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon OpenSearch Serverless to store product documents, index the SKU and secondary attributes, and query all catalog records through search APIs</strong> - Amazon OpenSearch Serverless can index product documents and support search use cases across many attributes. It can be useful when the application needs relevance ranking, full-text search, faceted navigation, or complex search expressions.</p>\n\n<p>However, the core access pattern requires dependable primary-key retrieval by SKU and flexible key-based queries by secondary attributes. DynamoDB with GSIs is better suited for high-volume operational item access with single-digit millisecond latency.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift Serverless to store product records in relational tables, distribute rows by SKU, and create materialized views for Department and Manufacturer access</strong> - Amazon Redshift Serverless can store and analyze large datasets without managing a provisioned cluster. Materialized views can accelerate repeated analytical query patterns.</p>\n\n<p>However, Redshift is an analytical warehouse, not the best operational store for high-volume product lookups during sudden spikes. It also does not provide the same flexible schema and on-demand request scaling model that DynamoDB provides for catalog access.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/on-demand-capacity-mode.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/on-demand-capacity-mode.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.CoreComponents.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.CoreComponents.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/GSI.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/GSI.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/</a></p>"},{"id":36,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A retail company processes daily sales reports uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket by regional store managers. The company uses a daily AWS Glue ETL pipeline to standardize and aggregate each report. The output is written to a file named SalesReport.csv in a reporting S3 bucket. Occasionally, a store manager uploads an incomplete report with missing transaction records or empty files. When this happens, the company must retain the previous day's report rather than overwrite it with bad data. A data engineer must ensure the previous day's report is overwritten only when the new report passes all data quality checks.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution meets this requirement with the least effort?</p>","answers":["<p>Configure the AWS Glue ETL pipeline to use AWS Glue Data Quality rules. Write rules in Data Quality Definition Language (DQDL) to detect empty files and missing values in required fields, and conditionally write output only when all rules pass</p>","<p>Use AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the ETL pipeline, adding a validation state that invokes a custom AWS Lambda function to perform field-level checks before allowing the pipeline to write to the reporting bucket only when all field-level checks are good</p>","<p>Trigger an AWS Lambda function after each upload to scan the file for missing transaction records and automatically populate missing fields using historical averages</p>","<p>Modify the AWS Glue ETL pipeline code in AWS Glue Studio to replace any missing transaction values with the most frequently occurring values for each field before writing output</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure the AWS Glue ETL pipeline to use AWS Glue Data Quality rules. Write rules in Data Quality Definition Language (DQDL) to detect empty files and missing values in required fields, and conditionally write output only when all rules pass</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue Data Quality with DQDL rules is the most native, least-effort solution for validating data within an existing Glue ETL pipeline. AWS Glue Data Quality is a built-in feature that allows engineers to define validation rules directly inside the Glue pipeline without writing custom code or provisioning additional services. DQDL (Data Quality Definition Language) provides a declarative syntax for expressing rules such as IsComplete \"transaction_id\" or RowCount &gt; 0, which map directly to the business requirements of detecting empty files and missing required fields. This means the engineer does not need to learn a new framework or maintain separate validation infrastructure.</p>\n\n<p>When Data Quality rules are configured in the Glue pipeline, the pipeline can be set to conditionally route data based on whether records pass or fail validation. Records that fail quality checks can be sent to a separate error path, while only fully validated data proceeds to overwrite the SalesReport.csv in the reporting bucket. This conditional branching is natively supported in AWS Glue Studio's visual interface, requiring minimal code changes to the existing pipeline. AWS documentation confirms that Glue Data Quality supports both row-level and dataset-level rules, making it suitable for catching both empty files and field-level missing values in a single configuration.</p>\n\n<p>The key advantage of this approach is that it operates entirely within the existing Glue ETL pipeline, requiring no additional AWS services, no custom Lambda code, and no changes to surrounding infrastructure. The data engineer simply adds a Data Quality node to the existing pipeline in Glue Studio and defines the DQDL rules. This satisfies the requirement of least effort while directly addressing the two failure conditions: empty files and missing required fields.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/da308d9457d08497d3cf3702f78cca63278858d7e2d4fcbcccdd6d42bcd02222.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/da308d9457d08497d3cf3702f78cca63278858d7e2d4fcbcccdd6d42bcd02222.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/22664b41dd3a28d76f36b306b21a03e68fde81994ef37b2865da686f0b2c7ba3.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/22664b41dd3a28d76f36b306b21a03e68fde81994ef37b2865da686f0b2c7ba3.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enable-strategic-data-quality-management-with-aws-glue-dqdl-labels/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enable-strategic-data-quality-management-with-aws-glue-dqdl-labels/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the ETL pipeline, adding a validation state that invokes a custom AWS Lambda function to perform field-level checks before allowing the pipeline to write to the reporting bucket only when all field-level checks are good</strong> - Orchestrating the pipeline with Step Functions adds significant architectural complexity. The engineer would need to define a new Step Functions state machine, integrate it with the existing Glue ETL job, write a custom Lambda validation function, handle retry logic and failure states, and manage IAM permissions across multiple services. While this is a valid enterprise-grade pattern for complex workflows, it represents substantial additional effort compared to simply adding a Data Quality node inside the existing Glue pipeline. Step Functions is better suited for multi-service workflows with complex branching logic, not for straightforward data validation within a single ETL job.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Trigger an AWS Lambda function after each upload to scan the file for missing transaction records and automatically populate missing fields using historical averages</strong> - While Lambda can be used to validate files after upload, this approach introduces significant additional effort compared to using native Glue Data Quality. The engineer would need to write, test, and deploy a custom Lambda function with logic to detect missing fields, compute historical averages for backfilling, and conditionally trigger or suppress the downstream ETL pipeline. This requires managing Lambda permissions, S3 event triggers, error handling, and potentially additional state management to track the previous day's file. None of this infrastructure exists today, making the setup overhead considerable.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Modify the AWS Glue ETL pipeline code in AWS Glue Studio to replace any missing transaction values with the most frequently occurring values for each field before writing output</strong> - This option fundamentally misunderstands the requirement. The business rule states that when data is missing, the previous day's file should be preserved - not that missing values should be fabricated or imputed. Replacing missing transaction values with the most common values would corrupt the report with inaccurate data, violating data integrity. Additionally, modifying ETL transformation code to handle imputation adds development and testing effort that is unnecessary given that Glue Data Quality already provides a purpose-built validation mechanism.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enable-strategic-data-quality-management-with-aws-glue-dqdl-labels/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enable-strategic-data-quality-management-with-aws-glue-dqdl-labels/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html</a></p>"},{"id":37,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A healthcare analytics company needs to deploy a multi-stage data processing pipeline for claims and provider data. Each stage of the pipeline must run transformation scripts, and the data engineering team needs to schedule and coordinate the stages as one workflow. The company must use only fully managed and serverless services for the pipeline.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to build the pipeline and operate Airflow DAGs that run the scripts on a schedule</p>","<p>Deploy AWS Glue jobs for the script-based stages and use AWS Glue workflows to schedule and orchestrate the pipeline</p>","<p>Use AWS Glue DataBrew to visually build the pipeline and use EventBridge Scheduler to run each preparation job on a schedule</p>","<p>Run containerized scripts on Amazon ECS tasks, store intermediate files in Amazon S3, and use Step Functions to coordinate the pipeline stages</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Deploy AWS Glue jobs for the script-based stages and use AWS Glue workflows to schedule and orchestrate the pipeline</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue jobs are designed to run ETL scripts without requiring the company to manage servers. A data engineer can write or generate PySpark, Spark, Python shell, or other supported job scripts depending on the processing requirement.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue workflows can coordinate multiple AWS Glue jobs, crawlers, and triggers as one ETL pipeline. The workflow can run jobs in a defined order, run independent stages in parallel, and start the pipeline on a schedule.</p>\n\n<p>This solution meets the requirement because it uses fully managed and serverless AWS Glue capabilities for both script execution and pipeline orchestration. The company does not need to manage EC2 instances, EMR clusters, Airflow environments, or custom container orchestration.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6648124feeae0dfdb9a2d102d9c82fa75edf8cf05ba4ae6b7da08eb841a02222.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/6648124feeae0dfdb9a2d102d9c82fa75edf8cf05ba4ae6b7da08eb841a02222.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/88662d2a9220109611a6448fe5153fd10b42000230bff03f6c98b60519a221ba.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/88662d2a9220109611a6448fe5153fd10b42000230bff03f6c98b60519a221ba.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run containerized scripts on Amazon ECS tasks, store intermediate files in Amazon S3, and use Step Functions to coordinate the pipeline stages</strong> - Amazon ECS tasks can run containerized scripts, and Step Functions can coordinate multi-stage workflows. This pattern can be valid for custom application workflows.</p>\n\n<p>However, this design requires the company to build and maintain container images, task definitions, networking configuration, and custom runtime packaging. AWS Glue jobs and workflows provide a more direct fully managed serverless ETL solution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue DataBrew to visually build the pipeline and use EventBridge Scheduler to run each preparation job on a schedule</strong> - AWS Glue DataBrew provides a visual data preparation experience and can run DataBrew jobs with low coding effort. It can be useful when users need interactive profiling and recipe-based transformations.</p>\n\n<p>However, DataBrew is not the best fit when each pipeline stage must run a script. AWS Glue jobs and workflows provide a better serverless ETL pattern for scripted, multi-stage pipelines.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to build the pipeline and operate Airflow DAGs that run the scripts on a schedule</strong> - Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow is a managed orchestration service for Apache Airflow. It can build and schedule complex workflows by using Airflow DAGs.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option does not provide the simplest serverless ETL execution pattern for script-based stages. AWS Glue jobs directly run ETL scripts in a serverless environment, and AWS Glue workflows can orchestrate those jobs without adding an Airflow layer.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/how-it-works.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/author-job-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/author-job-glue.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/</a></p>"},{"id":38,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A data engineer is tasked with using AWS services to ingest a dataset into an Amazon S3 data lake. Upon profiling the dataset manually, the engineer finds that it contains personally identifiable information (PII). The engineer needs to devise a method to both profile the dataset and mask the PII effectively.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following options can be independently used to fulfill this requirement with the least amount of operational effort? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>Ingest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Process the stream data using a Lambda function that masks the PII data before writing the output in Amazon S3</p>","<p>Utilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify and mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake</p>","<p>Ingest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Leverage a data transformation Lambda function to mask the PII data in the delivery stream and write the transformed output in Amazon S3</p>","<p>Run an AWS Glue DataBrew data profile job to identify and suggest potential PII columns present in the dataset. Execute a DataBrew job to apply the transformations to handle the sensitive columns in the entire dataset and store the masked data securely in Amazon S3</p>","<p>Utilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify the PII. Set up a rule in AWS Glue Data Quality to mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake</p>"],"correct":[1,3],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Utilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify and mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake</strong></p>\n\n<p>The Detect PII transform identifies Personal Identifiable Information (PII) in your data source. You choose the PII entity to identify, how you want the data to be scanned, and what to do with the PII entity that has been identified by the Detect PII transform.</p>\n\n<p>The Detect PII transform provides the ability to detect, mask, or remove entities that you define, or are pre-defined by AWS. This enables you to increase compliance and reduce liability. For example, you may want to ensure that no personally identifiable information exists in your data that can be read and want to mask social security numbers with a fixed string (such as xxx-xx-xxxx), phone numbers, or addresses. You can then use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2f51e227798a84a72d97c62b3e0b673c6e26cb37a0d076141bb5d5d53a6f9844.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/2f51e227798a84a72d97c62b3e0b673c6e26cb37a0d076141bb5d5d53a6f9844.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Run an AWS Glue DataBrew data profile job to identify and suggest potential PII columns present in the dataset. Execute a DataBrew job to apply the transformations to handle the sensitive columns in the entire dataset and store the masked data securely in Amazon S3</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue DataBrew, a visual data preparation tool, allows users to identify and handle sensitive data by applying advanced transformations like redaction, replacement, encryption, and decryption on their personally identifiable information (PII) data, and other types of data they deem sensitive. DataBrew has PII data handling transformations, which enable you to apply data masking, encryption, decryption, and other operations on your sensitive data.</p>\n\n<p>You can run a data profile job to identify and suggest potential PII columns present in a dataset. Then, you can target PII columns in a DataBrew project and apply various transformations to handle the sensitive columns existing in the dataset. Finally, run a DataBrew job to apply the transformations on the entire dataset and store the processed, masked, and encrypted data securely in Amazon S3.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/27c28e33b15db5262b0a0e6e93b086591408f7c0a23f4dfec7659940eec397fc.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/27c28e33b15db5262b0a0e6e93b086591408f7c0a23f4dfec7659940eec397fc.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ingest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Leverage a data transformation Lambda function to mask the PII data in the delivery stream and write the transformed output in Amazon S3</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Ingest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Process the stream data using a Lambda function that masks the PII data before writing the output in Amazon S3</strong></p>\n\n<p>You will need to write custom code in the Lambda function to mask the PII data, which represents unnecessary operational effort for the given use case, so both these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Utilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify the PII. Set up a rule in AWS Glue Data Quality to mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake</strong> - AWS Glue Data Quality is a feature of AWS Glue that reduces manual data quality effort by automatically measuring and monitoring the quality of data in data lakes and pipelines. In data lakes, it then automatically recommends data quality rules. You can modify these rules, add additional rules from built-in rule types, and configure actions to alert teams when quality issues occur.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue Data Quality currently supports 18 built-in rule types under four categories:</p>\n\n<p>Consistency rules check if data across different columns agrees by looking at column correlations.\nAccuracy rules check if record counts meet a set threshold and if columns are not empty, match certain patterns, have valid data types, and have valid values.\nIntegrity rules check if duplicates exist in a dataset.\nCompleteness rules check if data in your datasets do not have missing values.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue Data Quality does not have any pre-built rule to mask the PII. You could create a custom rule to mask the PII, but that involves additional operational effort, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-pii-data-identification-and-handling-using-aws-glue-databrew/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-pii-data-identification-and-handling-using-aws-glue-databrew/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html</a></p>"},{"id":39,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A legal services company stores signed case documents in an Amazon S3 bucket. Every object version must remain immutable for 6 years and must follow a write-once-read-many retention model. The company also needs to reduce storage cost for older documents. Any object version that is older than 15 months must be retrievable within 5 hours, and the company can permanently delete object versions after the 6-year retention period ends.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Enable S3 Versioning and S3 MFA Delete on the bucket, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and use Lifecycle expiration after 6 years</p>","<p>Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years</p>","<p>Enable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years</p>","<p>Enable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and expire versions after 6 years</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years</strong></p>\n\n<p>S3 Object Lock is required because the documents must use a write-once-read-many model and all object versions must remain immutable for the retention period. Object Lock prevents protected object versions from being overwritten or deleted until the retention period or legal hold no longer applies.</p>\n\n<p>S3 Object Lock works with S3 Versioning, so each version can be retained independently. The company can configure retention for object versions and use Lifecycle rules to manage storage transitions and expiration after the retention period has ended.</p>\n\n<p>S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is the correct archive storage class for the older document versions because the requirement says objects older than 15 months must be retrievable within 5 hours. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval supports retrieval options that can meet this access requirement, while still reducing storage cost for older data.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years</strong> - S3 Versioning preserves multiple versions of objects in a bucket. This helps recover from accidental overwrites or deletions and is an important foundation for version-level retention.</p>\n\n<p>However, versioning by itself does not enforce a WORM model. Users or roles with sufficient permissions could still delete versions unless Object Lock retention or legal holds protect them.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and expire versions after 6 years</strong> - S3 Object Lock correctly addresses the WORM and immutability requirement. S3 Intelligent-Tiering can also help optimize storage costs when access patterns change.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option transitions older object versions to S3 Glacier Deep Archive. Deep Archive is designed for long-term archival data with longer restore times, so it does not satisfy the requirement that older documents be accessible within 5 hours.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable S3 Versioning and S3 MFA Delete on the bucket, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and use Lifecycle expiration after 6 years</strong> - S3 MFA Delete adds protection for version deletion operations by requiring multi-factor authentication for certain delete actions. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval also meets the archive retrieval timing requirement.</p>\n\n<p>However, MFA Delete is not the same as S3 Object Lock. The requirement explicitly calls for all versions to be immutable and use a WORM model, so Object Lock is the required control.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock-managing.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock-managing.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/restoring-objects-retrieval-options.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/restoring-objects-retrieval-options.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html</a></p>"},{"id":40,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>The data engineering team at a retail company has set up a workflow to ingest the clickstream data into the raw zone of the S3 data lake. The team wants to run some SQL-based data sanity checks on the raw zone of the data lake.</p>\n\n<p>What AWS services would you recommend for this use-case such that the solution is cost-effective and easy to maintain?</p>","answers":["<p>Load the incremental raw zone data into RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks</p>","<p>Load the incremental raw zone data into an EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL-based sanity checks</p>","<p>Load the incremental raw zone data into Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks</p>","<p>Use Amazon Athena to run SQL-based analytics against S3 data</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena to run SQL-based analytics against S3 data</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Athena Benefits:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/athena/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Load the incremental raw zone data into Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks</strong> - Amazon Redshift is a fully managed petabyte-scale cloud-based data warehouse product designed for large-scale data set storage and analysis.\nAs the development team would have to maintain and monitor the Redshift cluster size and would require significant development time to set up the processes to consume the data periodically, so this option is ruled out.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Load the incremental raw zone data into an EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL-based sanity checks</strong> - Amazon EMR is the industry-leading cloud big data platform for processing vast amounts of data using open source tools such as Apache Spark, Apache Hive, Apache HBase, Apache Flink, Apache Hudi, and Presto. Amazon EMR uses Hadoop, an open-source framework, to distribute your data and processing across a resizable cluster of Amazon EC2 instances.\nUsing an EMR cluster would imply managing the underlying infrastructure so it’s ruled out because the correct solution for the given use case should require the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Load the incremental raw zone data into RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks</strong> - Loading the incremental data into RDS implies data migration jobs will have to be written via a Lambda function or an EC2-based process. This goes against the requirement that the solution should involve the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance. Hence this option is not correct.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/athena/</a></p>"},{"id":41,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A regional insurance company stores historical policyholder records in an Amazon Redshift table. A column named ContactEmail contains null values, malformed strings, and some valid email addresses. Several downstream notification and compliance workflows depend on the quality of the ContactEmail column. A data engineer must define an AWS Glue Data Quality rule that fails when fewer than 90% of the values in the ContactEmail column are valid email addresses.</p>\n\n<p>Which component of an AWS Glue Data Quality rule will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>ColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold &gt; 0.1</p>","<p>ColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold &gt; 0.9</p>","<p>Uniqueness \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold &gt; 0.9</p>","<p>Completeness \"ContactEmail\" &gt; 0.9</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>ColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold &gt; 0.9</strong></p>\n\n<p>The ColumnValues rule type evaluates whether values in a specified column satisfy a condition. In this case, the rule checks whether values in the ContactEmail column match the email-style pattern.</p>\n\n<p>The with threshold clause specifies the percentage of rows that must satisfy the rule condition. Setting the threshold to &gt; 0.9 means the rule passes only when more than 90% of the values in the ContactEmail column match the pattern.</p>\n\n<p>This rule directly meets the requirement because the company needs to validate the percentage of acceptable values in one column. Null entries and malformed values will not satisfy the matching condition, so they reduce the valid percentage and can cause the rule to fail when the valid percentage drops below the required level.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/aae4f5a94ee4a8630e3dfd094e8b90faff08cb8204983087ea45b22bce18d837.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/aae4f5a94ee4a8630e3dfd094e8b90faff08cb8204983087ea45b22bce18d837.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-data-quality-is-generally-available/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-data-quality-is-generally-available/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Uniqueness \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold &gt; 0.9</strong> - The Uniqueness rule type evaluates whether values in a column are unique. It is useful when a column should not contain duplicate values, such as identifiers or keys.</p>\n\n<p>This option does not validate whether values are email addresses. A column can contain many unique invalid strings and still pass a uniqueness-focused rule, so this option does not meet the requirement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>ColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold &gt; 0.1</strong> - This option uses the correct ColumnValues rule type and applies a matches condition to the ContactEmail column. However, the threshold is too low for the stated requirement.</p>\n\n<p>A threshold of &gt; 0.1 means the rule can pass when only slightly more than 10% of values match the pattern. The company needs the rule to fail when fewer than 90% of values are valid, so the threshold must be greater than 0.9.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Completeness \"ContactEmail\" &gt; 0.9</strong> - The Completeness rule type checks whether values are present and not null. It can help detect missing values in the ContactEmail column.</p>\n\n<p>However, completeness alone does not determine whether non-null values are valid email addresses. A malformed string can be complete but still fail the email-format requirement.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ColumnValues.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ColumnValues.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-gs-studio.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-gs-studio.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-data-quality-is-generally-available/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-data-quality-is-generally-available/</a></p>"},{"id":42,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A data engineer is building a 140-TB transactional data lake in Amazon S3 by using Apache Iceberg tables. The data engineer wants to run AWS Glue Apache Spark jobs that use the Iceberg framework to process the data lake. The jobs must support large-scale processing while reducing the need to manually tune worker capacity.</p>\n\n<p>Which combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>Enable the <code>--job-bookmark-option</code> parameter with job-bookmark-enable so AWS Glue tracks Iceberg table commits and enables Iceberg support</p>","<p>Set the <code>--enable-auto-scaling</code> AWS Glue job parameter to true</p>","<p>Set iceberg as the value for the <code>--datalake-formats</code> AWS Glue job parameter</p>","<p>Create a DynamoDB table for Iceberg metadata tracking and configure the AWS Glue job to use DynamoDB as the primary Iceberg catalog</p>","<p>Create an empty <code>--conf</code> key for the AWS Glue job and rely on AWS Glue to infer all Apache Iceberg Spark settings automatically</p>"],"correct":[1,2],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set iceberg as the value for the <code>--datalake-formats</code> AWS Glue job parameter</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue provides native support for Apache Iceberg as a data lake framework. To enable that native support in an AWS Glue Apache Spark job, the data engineer must set the <code>--datalake-formats</code> job parameter to iceberg.</p>\n\n<p>This parameter tells AWS Glue to include the supported Iceberg libraries in the job runtime. The Spark job can then use the Iceberg framework to read from and write to Iceberg tables in the data lake.</p>\n\n<p>This step is required because the job must use the Iceberg framework. Without enabling the Iceberg data lake format, the AWS Glue Spark job does not automatically include the native Iceberg integration that the workload needs.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/08138511cf7a23772cfab0b23bebf7131fabe5dc8d82cc877367a9821a9db07d.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/08138511cf7a23772cfab0b23bebf7131fabe5dc8d82cc877367a9821a9db07d.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-iceberg.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-iceberg.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Set the <code>--enable-auto-scaling</code> AWS Glue job parameter to true</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue Auto Scaling helps large Spark jobs adjust worker capacity based on workload demand. For a 140-TB data lake, processing requirements can vary significantly across tables, partitions, and job stages.</p>\n\n<p>Setting <code>--enable-auto-scaling</code> to true allows AWS Glue to scale the job resources instead of requiring the data engineer to manually size the job for every run. This helps reduce manual tuning while supporting large-scale Spark processing.</p>\n\n<p>This step complements the Iceberg configuration. The <code>--datalake-formats</code> parameter enables the Iceberg framework, and Auto Scaling helps the Glue job process the large data lake more efficiently with less manual capacity management.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an empty <code>--conf</code> key for the AWS Glue job and rely on AWS Glue to infer all Apache Iceberg Spark settings automatically</strong> - The <code>--conf</code> parameter can configure Spark settings for AWS Glue jobs. In Iceberg workloads, Spark configuration can be useful when the job needs explicit catalog or extension settings.</p>\n\n<p>The issue with this option is that creating an empty <code>--conf</code> key does not configure anything. It does not enable the Iceberg framework, and it does not replace the required <code>--datalake-formats</code> iceberg setting.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a DynamoDB table for Iceberg metadata tracking and configure the AWS Glue job to use DynamoDB as the primary Iceberg catalog</strong> - DynamoDB can support scalable key-value access patterns and can be used in some data lake architectures for state or locking patterns.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because DynamoDB is not the required step to enable Apache Iceberg in AWS Glue Spark jobs. The required steps are to enable the Iceberg data lake format and enable Auto Scaling for large-scale processing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable the <code>--job-bookmark-option</code> parameter with job-bookmark-enable so AWS Glue tracks Iceberg table commits and enables Iceberg support</strong> - AWS Glue job bookmarks can track previously processed data for supported sources. They are useful for incremental ETL workflows that need to avoid reprocessing the same input files.</p>\n\n<p>This does not enable Iceberg support. Job bookmarks do not add the Iceberg framework to the Spark runtime, and they do not provide the capacity scaling required for a 140-TB data lake workload.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-iceberg.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-iceberg.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/apache-iceberg-on-aws/iceberg-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/apache-iceberg-on-aws/iceberg-glue.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-glue-arguments.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-glue-arguments.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/auto-scaling.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/auto-scaling.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-auto-scaling-automatically-resize-serverless-computing-resources-for-lower-cost-with-optimized-apache-spark/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-auto-scaling-automatically-resize-serverless-computing-resources-for-lower-cost-with-optimized-apache-spark/</a></p>"},{"id":43,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A data engineer is tuning an AWS Glue Apache Spark ETL job that joins two large customer activity datasets. The job uses a sort-merge join between two equally sized Spark DataFrames.</p>\n\n<p>During the shuffle stage, the job fails with the following error: No space left on device. The data engineer must resolve the failure without changing the join to a small-table optimization.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will resolve the error?</p>","answers":["<p>Convert the sort-merge join to a broadcast join so Spark sends one DataFrame to all executors before joining</p>","<p>Convert the Spark DataFrames to AWS Glue DynamicFrames and perform a DynamicFrame join so AWS Glue can avoid local shuffle storage</p>","<p>Enable the AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager so the job writes shuffle files and spill data to Amazon S3</p>","<p>Create an AWS Glue crawler for both source datasets and update the Data Catalog before the job runs so Spark can reduce shuffle disk usage</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable the AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager so the job writes shuffle files and spill data to Amazon S3</strong></p>\n\n<p>The job fails because a sort-merge join of two large DataFrames can create a large shuffle. Spark writes shuffle and spill data to local disk on the workers, and the job can fail with a No space left on device error when the local disk is exhausted.</p>\n\n<p>The AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager can use Amazon S3 for Spark shuffle storage. By writing shuffle files and spill data to S3, the job is no longer limited only by the local disk capacity of the AWS Glue workers during the large shuffle stage.</p>\n\n<p>This solution directly addresses the root cause of the error. The issue is not the DataFrame API, catalog metadata, or timeout behavior. The job needs a shuffle storage configuration that can handle the volume of intermediate data produced by the large sort-merge join.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/079afe72d2d0a1a3741f9668480a11b1f22f9e336d87b3ce583bd271aca651f4.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/079afe72d2d0a1a3741f9668480a11b1f22f9e336d87b3ce583bd271aca651f4.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0842aad8cb085d67098fde31c7fc78f71125539dcd26c2996091a9cbd06837d9.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/0842aad8cb085d67098fde31c7fc78f71125539dcd26c2996091a9cbd06837d9.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Convert the Spark DataFrames to AWS Glue DynamicFrames and perform a DynamicFrame join so AWS Glue can avoid local shuffle storage</strong> - AWS Glue DynamicFrames provide AWS Glue-specific abstractions for semi-structured data and ETL transformations. They can be useful when working with schema flexibility, choice types, and AWS Glue transformations.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that converting DataFrames to DynamicFrames does not remove the need to shuffle data for a large join. A DynamicFrame join over two large datasets can still require substantial shuffle processing and can still hit disk limits.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an AWS Glue crawler for both source datasets and update the Data Catalog before the job runs so Spark can reduce shuffle disk usage</strong> - AWS Glue crawlers can discover schemas and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This helps ETL jobs and query engines understand table metadata and source layouts.</p>\n\n<p>Catalog updates do not solve local disk exhaustion during a Spark shuffle. The failure occurs while Spark executes the sort-merge join, so the appropriate fix is to move shuffle storage to Amazon S3 by using the AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Convert the sort-merge join to a broadcast join so Spark sends one DataFrame to all executors before joining</strong> - A broadcast join can improve performance when one side of the join is small enough to fit in executor memory. Spark sends the smaller dataset to all executors and avoids a large shuffle for that side.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because the question states that both DataFrames are large and equally sized. Broadcasting a large DataFrame can exhaust memory and is not the correct optimization for this join.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-shuffle-manager.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-shuffle-manager.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/optimize-shuffles.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/optimize-shuffles.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/glue-no-spaces-on-device-error\">https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/glue-no-spaces-on-device-error</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":44,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>An energy company collects hourly telemetry from thousands of smart meters into an Amazon S3 data lake. Each record includes fields such as meter type, device identifier, service condition, reading timestamp, energy zone, and facility location. The dataset grows by several million records each day. Analysts run frequent Amazon Athena queries to compare performance trends for specific meter types across time ranges.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the FASTEST query execution time?</p>","answers":["<p>Store the telemetry data in Apache Parquet format, partition the dataset by meter type, and sort the records by reading timestamp</p>","<p>Store the telemetry data in JSON format, partition the dataset by facility location, and sort the records by device identifier for all downstream Athena queries</p>","<p>Store the telemetry data in Apache ORC format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by meter type</p>","<p>Store the telemetry data in CSV format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by service condition</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the telemetry data in Apache Parquet format, partition the dataset by meter type, and sort the records by reading timestamp</strong></p>\n\n<p>Apache Parquet is a columnar storage format that improves Athena query performance by allowing Athena to read only the columns required by a query. This reduces the volume of data scanned from Amazon S3 compared with row-based formats such as CSV or JSON.</p>\n\n<p>Partitioning the dataset by meter type aligns with the query pattern because analysts frequently run queries for specific meter types. When the query includes the meter type partition column in the WHERE clause, Athena can prune irrelevant partitions and scan only the relevant subset of S3 objects.</p>\n\n<p>Sorting the Parquet data by reading timestamp further improves query efficiency for time-range analysis. Columnar formats such as Parquet store statistics that can help query engines skip unnecessary data blocks, so sorting by a commonly filtered range column can reduce scan work and improve execution time.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/4ec01f8798952144ec6e16642f20e6df70601b413aaceb977b3bb71de999d026.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/4ec01f8798952144ec6e16642f20e6df70601b413aaceb977b3bb71de999d026.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the telemetry data in CSV format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by service condition</strong> - CSV is a row-based text format and is less efficient for Athena analytical queries than Parquet or ORC. Athena often needs to scan more data from S3 when querying CSV because the format does not provide the same column pruning benefits.</p>\n\n<p>Partitioning by reading date can help date-filtered queries, but the primary query pattern focuses on specific meter types. Sorting by service condition also does not align with the stated analytics pattern.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the telemetry data in Apache ORC format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by meter type</strong> - Apache ORC is a columnar format and can perform well with Athena. Sorting by meter type can also help when queries filter or group by meter type.</p>\n\n<p>However, partitioning by reading date is less aligned with the stated requirement than partitioning by meter type. Since analysts frequently query performance trends for specific meter types, partitioning on meter type allows Athena to eliminate more irrelevant data early in the query plan.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the telemetry data in JSON format, partition the dataset by facility location, and sort the records by device identifier for all downstream Athena queries</strong> - JSON is a flexible text format, but it is not optimized for high-performance Athena analytics at large scale. It typically scans more data than columnar formats and can increase query latency and cost.</p>\n\n<p>Partitioning by facility location does not match the query pattern that focuses on meter type. Sorting by device identifier also helps individual-device lookups more than meter-type performance analysis.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-s3-throttling-optimizing-your-tables.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-s3-throttling-optimizing-your-tables.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/</a></p>"},{"id":45,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A language learning institute is building an AI tutoring application that reviews student writing assignments. The application must provide individualized feedback that is grounded in the institute's approved course books and study guides. The application must support submissions in several languages, return feedback in the learner's selected language, and include citations that point to the relevant sections of the course materials.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Host a custom fine-tuned large language model on Amazon SageMaker AI, train the model on the institute's course books, and generate feedback with section references</p>","<p>Create an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for keyword search across course materials, run Amazon Comprehend entity detection on each essay, pass matching textbook paragraphs to an Amazon Bedrock model, and use custom prompt templates to generate multilingual feedback and manually formatted citations</p>","<p>Create an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base from the institute's course materials and configure a multilingual foundation model to generate feedback with source citations</p>","<p>Use Amazon Comprehend to identify the essay language and topics, use Amazon Kendra to search course materials, and use an AWS Lambda function to assemble feedback with references</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base from the institute's course materials and configure a multilingual foundation model to generate feedback with source citations</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases provides a managed retrieval augmented generation capability. The institute can ingest approved course books and study guides into a knowledge base, and the application can retrieve the most relevant source chunks when a student submits an essay.</p>\n\n<p>The RetrieveAndGenerate capability combines retrieval from the knowledge base with foundation model response generation. It can return generated responses and citations that reference the retrieved source chunks. This helps the application ground feedback in the institute's approved materials instead of relying only on the model's general knowledge.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational overhead because Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases manages the ingestion, chunking, embedding, storage integration, retrieval workflow, and citation support. The institute can select an appropriate multilingual foundation model and instruct it to respond in the student's selected language without building and maintaining a custom vector database, custom retrieval layer, or fine-tuned model hosting platform.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/5c2139f8c6837374412c2ab12862b33f8a5d32d004af82277e99a696ab31d76e.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/5c2139f8c6837374412c2ab12862b33f8a5d32d004af82277e99a696ab31d76e.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-it-works.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-it-works.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Comprehend to identify the essay language and topics, use Amazon Kendra to search course materials, and use an AWS Lambda function to assemble feedback with references</strong> - Amazon Comprehend can detect language, extract key phrases, and identify entities or sentiment. Amazon Kendra can search enterprise content and return relevant documents or passages.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option requires the institute to build the feedback generation and citation formatting workflow with Lambda. It does not provide the same managed RAG workflow and integrated generated-response citations that Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases provides.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Host a custom fine-tuned large language model on Amazon SageMaker AI, train the model on the institute's course books, and generate feedback with section references</strong> - Amazon SageMaker AI can host custom machine learning models and can support model training and fine-tuning workflows. However, fine-tuning a model on textbooks does not automatically provide reliable citations to specific textbook sections in generated responses.</p>\n\n<p>This approach also adds significant operational overhead. The institute would need to manage model training data, fine-tuning jobs, hosting endpoints, scaling, evaluation, and citation generation logic instead of using a managed knowledge base with retrieval and citations.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for keyword search across course materials, run Amazon Comprehend entity detection on each essay, pass matching textbook paragraphs to an Amazon Bedrock model, and use custom prompt templates to generate multilingual feedback and manually formatted citations</strong> - Amazon OpenSearch Service can support search use cases, and Amazon Comprehend can identify useful signals from essay text. This architecture could help retrieve candidate textbook passages.</p>\n\n<p>However, the institute would still need to build custom retrieval orchestration, prompt construction, multilingual response handling, and citation formatting. This makes the solution more operationally complex than using Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases with native retrieval and citation support.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/knowledge-base.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/knowledge-base.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-it-works.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-it-works.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-retrieval.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-retrieval.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-test-retrieve-generate.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-test-retrieve-generate.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-a-contextual-chatbot-application-using-knowledge-bases-for-amazon-bedrock/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-a-contextual-chatbot-application-using-knowledge-bases-for-amazon-bedrock/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/evaluate-and-improve-performance-of-amazon-bedrock-knowledge-bases/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/evaluate-and-improve-performance-of-amazon-bedrock-knowledge-bases/</a></p>"},{"id":46,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A company intends to organize its Amazon S3 storage used for a data lake by implementing partitioning that has the S3 object keys in the format: s3://bucket/prefix/year=2023/month=01/day=01. A data engineer is tasked with ensuring that the AWS Glue Data Catalog remains updated in sync with any new partitions added to the S3 bucket.</p>\n\n<p>What solution would best meet these requirements while minimizing latency?</p>","answers":["<p>Update the existing code that writes data to Amazon S3, so that it also invokes the Boto3 AWS Glue create_partition API call</p>","<p>Configure an AWS Glue crawler to run on a daily schedule</p>","<p>Schedule an AWS Lambda function to run the AWS Glue CreatePartition API twice each day</p>","<p>Manually execute the MSCK REPAIR TABLE command from the AWS Glue console on demand</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Update the existing code that writes data to Amazon S3, so that it also invokes the Boto3 AWS Glue create_partition API call</strong></p>\n\n<p>Partitioning is an important technique for organizing datasets so they can be queried efficiently. It organizes data in a hierarchical directory structure based on the distinct values of one or more columns. For example, you might decide to partition your application logs in Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) by date, broken down by year, month, and day. Files that correspond to a single day's worth of data are then placed under a prefix such as s3://my_bucket/logs/year=2018/month=01/day=23/.</p>\n\n<p>Partitioning means organizing data into directories (or \"prefixes\") on Amazon S3 based on a particular property of the data. Such properties are called partition keys. A common partition key is the date or some other unit of time such as the year or month. However, a dataset can be partitioned by more than one key. For example, data about product sales might be partitioned by date, product category, and market.</p>\n\n<p>For the given use case, you need to update the existing code that writes data to Amazon S3, so that it also invokes the Boto3 AWS Glue create_partition API call.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure an AWS Glue crawler to run on a daily schedule</strong> - A daily schedule to invoke the Glue Crawler invites severe latency into the solution. So, this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Manually execute the MSCK REPAIR TABLE command from the AWS Glue console on demand</strong> - This option has the worst outcome in terms of latency, so this is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Schedule an AWS Lambda function to run the AWS Glue CreatePartition API twice each day</strong> - A schedule that invokes a Lambda function twice a day run the AWS Glue CreatePartition API will have siginificant latency to sync any new partitions. So, this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/reference/services/glue/client/create_partition.html\">https://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/reference/services/glue/client/create_partition.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-add-partition.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-add-partition.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/</a></p>"},{"id":47,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A large SQL Server database on RDS contains historical transaction data. A process is needed to automate the following requirements: regularly identify data older than 3 months, and export the old data to an S3 bucket for long-term, cost-effective storage.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following options can be used to automate this export from SQL Server to S3 and perform lifecycle management in Amazon S3 to build a solution with the least operational overhead? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>For cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering</p>","<p>For cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive</p>","<p>Write an AWS Lambda function that identifies and exports old data from RDS to S3. Trigger the Lambda function through Amazon CloudWatch RDS Events</p>","<p>Configure AWS Step Functions to create an automation workflow. The workflow will call DataSync to sync the database files to the Amazon S3 bucket</p>","<p>Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to set up a task that runs periodically to migrate data older than 3 months from your RDS instance to an S3 bucket</p>"],"correct":[1,4],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to set up a task that runs periodically to migrate data older than 3 months from your RDS instance to an S3 bucket</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>For cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) is a managed migration and replication service that helps you move your databases and analytics workloads to AWS quickly and securely. The source database remains fully operational during the migration, minimizing downtime to applications that rely on the database.</p>\n\n<p>Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to set up a task that runs periodically (e.g. weekly) to migrate data older than 3 months from your RDS instance to an S3 bucket. DMS supports homogeneous migrations between SQL Server and S3.</p>\n\n<p>For cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/d9d3995c4e49124cba20dea02fbf8ce91a2f929eb9acf80e132267e61f07c13d.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/d9d3995c4e49124cba20dea02fbf8ce91a2f929eb9acf80e132267e61f07c13d.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Write an AWS Lambda function that identifies and exports old data from RDS to S3. Trigger the Lambda function through Amazon CloudWatch RDS Events</strong> - An RDS event indicates a change in the Amazon RDS environment. RDS events cannot be triggered for data changes. Amazon RDS records events that relate to the following resources: DB instances, DB parameter groups, DB security groups, DB snapshots, RDS Proxy events, and Blue/green deployment events.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure AWS Step Functions to create an automation workflow. The workflow will call DataSync to sync the database files to the Amazon S3 bucket</strong> - This is unnecessarily complex. Also, the step function itself will need a trigger.</p>\n\n<p><strong>For cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering</strong> - The S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class is designed to optimize storage costs by automatically moving data to the most cost-effective access tier when access patterns change, without operational overhead or impact on performance. S3 Intelligent-Tiering is not suited for long-term archival of data.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://repost.aws/questions/QUA-X-OBSIS36vgqSoS1srbw/efficiently-archiving-transactional-data-in-rds-mssql-to-s3\">https://repost.aws/questions/QUA-X-OBSIS36vgqSoS1srbw/efficiently-archiving-transactional-data-in-rds-mssql-to-s3</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dms/\">https://aws.amazon.com/dms/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html</a></p>"},{"id":48,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A company uses Amazon S3 buckets to store sensitive customer data that is business-critical. The data is kept segregated and well organized to ensure low latency. Recently, the data engineering team noticed that the lifecycle policies on the Amazon S3 buckets have not been applied optimally, resulting in higher costs.</p>\n\n<p>Can you recommend a solution to reduce storage costs on Amazon S3 while keeping the team's involvement to a minimum?</p>","answers":["<p>Configure Amazon EFS to provide a fast, cost-effective, and sharable storage service</p>","<p>Use Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access, to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage</p>","<p>Use Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class to optimize the Amazon S3 storage costs</p>","<p>Use Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class to optimize the Amazon S3 storage costs</strong></p>\n\n<p>The Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class is designed to optimize costs by automatically moving data to the most cost-effective access tier, without performance impact or operational overhead. It works by storing objects in two access tiers: one tier that is optimized for frequent access and another lower-cost tier that is optimized for infrequent access.</p>\n\n<p>For a small monthly monitoring and automation fee per object, Amazon S3 monitors access patterns of the objects in Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering and moves the ones that have not been accessed for 30 consecutive days to the infrequent access tier. If an object in the infrequent access tier is accessed, it is automatically moved back to the frequent access tier. There are no retrieval fees when using the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class, and no additional tiering fees when objects are moved between access tiers. It is the ideal storage class for long-lived data with access patterns that are unknown or unpredictable.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 Storage Classes can be configured at the object level and a single bucket can contain objects stored in Amazon S3 Standard, Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering, Amazon S3 Standard-IA, and Amazon S3 One Zone-IA. You can upload objects directly to Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering, or use S3 Lifecycle policies to transfer objects from Amazon S3 Standard and Amazon S3 Standard-IA to Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering. You can also archive objects from Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering to Amazon S3 Glacier.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure Amazon EFS to provide a fast, cost-effective, and sharable storage service</strong> - Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) provides a simple, scalable, fully managed elastic NFS file system for use with AWS Cloud services and on-premises resources. Amazon EFS offers sharable service, unlike Amazon Elastic Block Storage (EBS) which cannot be shared by instances. Amazon EFS is costlier than storing data in Amazon S3. Also, Amazon EFS needs an Amazon EC2 instance or an AWS Direct Connect network connection. Hence, this is not the correct option.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access, to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage</strong> - Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Unlike other Amazon S3 Storage Classes which store data in a minimum of three Availability Zones (AZs), Amazon S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single AZ and costs 20% less than Amazon S3 Standard-IA. Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is ideal for customers who want a lower-cost option for infrequently accessed data but do not require the availability and resilience of Amazon S3 Standard or Amazon S3 Standard-IA. This is not the right option, since the data stored is business-critical and cannot be risked by using Amazon S3 One Zone-IA.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage</strong> - Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class has a first-byte latency of several hours, so this option is incorrect for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a></p>"},{"id":49,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A company uses AWS services such as Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3 as well as their on-premises SQL Server database to store the consumer data. The company also uses Salesforce as its SaaS application. The company wants to build a dashboard that will help the managers visualize the data points from all these systems.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following represents a simple and easy way to build the dashboard in the least possible time?</p>","answers":["<p>Use AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Configure AWS Glue Data Catalog to connect the S3 data to Amazon Athena for further analysis and visualizations. Use a Glue Crawler to automate the process</p>","<p>Use AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to generate the visualizations needed for the dashboards</p>","<p>Configure Amazon QuickSight to connect to the data sources and generate the visualizations needed for the dashboard</p>","<p>Use the federated queries feature of Amazon Athena to join the different data sources and visualize the data using Amazon QuickSight</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure Amazon QuickSight to connect to the data sources and generate the visualizations needed for the dashboard</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon QuickSight is a very fast, easy-to-use, cloud-powered business analytics service that makes it easy for all employees within an organization to build visualizations, perform ad-hoc analysis, and quickly get business insights from their data, anytime, on any device.</p>\n\n<p>QuickSight can connect to AWS data sources including Amazon RDS, Amazon Aurora, Amazon Redshift, Amazon Athena, and Amazon S3. You can also upload Excel spreadsheets or flat files (CSV, TSV, CLF, and ELF), connect to on-premises databases like SQL Server, MySQL, and PostgreSQL, and import data from SaaS applications like Salesforce.</p>\n\n<p>QuickSight Key Features:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/0c7dc34728a33d844935dc64b4ac31f7c1c4abfc1461f4a6cce6dd9c65e4f00c.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/0c7dc34728a33d844935dc64b4ac31f7c1c4abfc1461f4a6cce6dd9c65e4f00c.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/quicksight/\">https://aws.amazon.com/quicksight/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use the federated queries feature of Amazon Athena to join the different data sources and visualize the data using Amazon QuickSight</strong> - Amazon Athena supports federated query, a feature that allows you to query data in sources other than Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3). You can use federated queries in Athena to query the data in place or build pipelines that extract data from multiple data sources and store them in Amazon S3.</p>\n\n<p>You can use different connectors to run federated queries with complex joins across different data sources with Athena and visualize the data with Amazon QuickSight. This option requires significant query development effort to join the data sources, so it's not the right fit for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to generate the visualizations needed for the dashboards</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Configure AWS Glue Data Catalog to connect the S3 data to Amazon Athena for further analysis and visualizations. Use a Glue Crawler to automate the process</strong></p>\n\n<p>Both these options require a lot of heavy lifting to migrate data from the different sources into S3. These options are unnecessarily complex and hence not the best fit for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/quicksight/resources/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/quicksight/resources/faqs/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/data-sources-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/data-sources-glue.html</a></p>"},{"id":50,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>An AWS Glue job is scheduled to be run every Sunday. The Glue job copies data from certain folders in an S3 bucket to Redshift. To prevent reprocessing of old data, job bookmarks have been enabled on the AWS Glue job. However, the ETL job is reprocessing data that was already processed in an earlier run.</p>\n\n<p>What could be the underlying issue and how should it be fixed to stop reprocessing of data? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>You have multiple concurrent jobs with job bookmarks, and the max concurrency isn't set to 1</p>","<p>The job.commit() object is missing</p>","<p>You have added a transformation context parameter to the DynamicFrame referenced within the job, which is causing the job to crash</p>","<p>Job bookmarks do not work for Amazon S3 input sources</p>","<p>AWS Glue keeps track of job bookmarks by storing the metadata in Amazon S3 configured during job creation. Deleting the S3 bucket can result in job reprocessing</p>"],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>You have multiple concurrent jobs with job bookmarks, and the max concurrency isn't set to 1</strong> - Ensure that the maximum number of concurrent runs for the job is 1. When you have multiple concurrent jobs with job bookmarks and the maximum concurrency is set to 1, the job bookmark doesn't work correctly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The job.commit() object is missing</strong> - Ensure that your job run script ends with the following commit: job.commit(). When you include this object, AWS Glue records the timestamp and path of the job run. If you run the job again with the same path, AWS Glue processes only the new files. If you don't include this object and job bookmarks are enabled, the job reprocesses the already processed files along with the new files and creates redundancy in the job's target data store.</p>\n\n<p>Troubleshooting reprocessing error in job bookmarks:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/dbf37fc5bea42c52e08d60957a7001cde25508b5861be789d0de7eeb780ddaec.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/dbf37fc5bea42c52e08d60957a7001cde25508b5861be789d0de7eeb780ddaec.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-troubleshooting-errors.html#error-job-bookmarks-reprocess-data\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-troubleshooting-errors.html#error-job-bookmarks-reprocess-data</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Job bookmarks do not work for Amazon S3 input sources</strong> - This statement is incorrect. Job bookmarks can be configured even when the input source is Amazon S3.</p>\n\n<p><strong>You have added a transformation context parameter to the DynamicFrame referenced within the job, which is causing the job to crash</strong> - Transformation context is an optional parameter in the GlueContext class, but job bookmarks don't work if you don't include it. So this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>AWS Glue keeps track of job bookmarks by storing the metadata in Amazon S3 configured during job creation. Deleting the S3 bucket can result in job reprocessing</strong> - This statement is incorrect. AWS Glue keeps track of job bookmarks with the job itself. If you delete a job, the job bookmark is deleted.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-troubleshooting-errors.html#error-job-bookmarks-reprocess-data\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-troubleshooting-errors.html#error-job-bookmarks-reprocess-data</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuations.html</a></p>"},{"id":51,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A data engineer supports several AWS Glue Apache Spark ETL jobs that transform daily advertising and clickstream datasets. The company has enabled monitoring and logging for all AWS Glue jobs.</p>\n\n<p>One Spark job starts failing during a transformation stage. The data engineer wants to investigate the failure by reviewing detailed metrics for each individual Spark stage, including task progress, stage timing, and shuffle behavior.</p>\n\n<p>How can the data engineer access the stage-level metrics?</p>","answers":["<p>Query the AWS Glue Data Catalog tables that the job reads and compare table metadata changes with the failed stage execution</p>","<p>Open the AWS Glue Spark UI for the job run and review the job, stage, task, DAG, and execution details</p>","<p>Use the AWS Glue job run insights feature to examine every Spark stage metric and task timeline for the failed run</p>","<p>Review Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the AWS Glue job and use the Glue namespace to inspect every individual Spark stage</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Open the AWS Glue Spark UI for the job run and review the job, stage, task, DAG, and execution details</strong></p>\n\n<p>The AWS Glue Spark UI is the correct place to inspect detailed Spark execution information for AWS Glue Apache Spark jobs. It provides visibility into Spark jobs, stages, tasks, directed acyclic graphs, and execution timelines.</p>\n\n<p>This is useful when a job fails in a specific transformation stage. The data engineer can use the Spark UI to identify slow stages, failed tasks, shuffle behavior, skew, input and output sizes, and other details that help explain why the Spark job failed.</p>\n\n<p>This solution directly meets the requirement because the engineer needs metrics for individual stages within the job. AWS Glue monitoring and CloudWatch metrics provide useful operational visibility, but Spark UI provides the detailed stage-level Spark execution view.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Query the AWS Glue Data Catalog tables that the job reads and compare table metadata changes with the failed stage execution</strong> - AWS Glue Data Catalog stores metadata about databases, tables, partitions, and schemas. Reviewing catalog metadata can help identify schema or partition issues that might affect a job.</p>\n\n<p>The issue with this approach is that catalog metadata does not contain Spark runtime stage metrics. It cannot show task progress, failed Spark stages, shuffle details, or execution timelines for a job run.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Review Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the AWS Glue job and use the Glue namespace to inspect every individual Spark stage</strong> - Amazon CloudWatch can show AWS Glue job metrics and observability metrics. These metrics are helpful for monitoring job health, resource usage, failures, and operational trends.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that CloudWatch does not provide the full Spark UI view of every individual Spark stage and task. For detailed stage inspection, the engineer should use the AWS Glue Spark UI.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use the AWS Glue job run insights feature to examine every Spark stage metric and task timeline for the failed run</strong> - AWS Glue job run insights can help diagnose job failures and provide summarized information to speed up troubleshooting. It is useful for identifying common failure patterns and probable causes.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because run insights is not the primary interface for detailed Spark stage metrics and task timelines. The Spark UI is the purpose-built tool for reviewing individual Spark stages.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-ui.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-ui.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-ui-jobs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-ui-jobs.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-job-insights.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-job-insights.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/investigate.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/investigate.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-studio-serverless-spark-ui-for-better-monitoring-and-troubleshooting/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-studio-serverless-spark-ui-for-better-monitoring-and-troubleshooting/</a></p>"},{"id":52,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A data engineer is tasked with managing the real-time ingestion of streaming data into AWS. The solution must have the capability to perform real-time analytics, including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes with high fault tolerance.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following options fulfills these criteria while minimizing the operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Leverage Amazon Kinesis Firehose with a data transformation Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes</p>","<p>Leverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes</p>","<p>Leverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer app running on an EC2 instance to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes</p>","<p>Leverage Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes</strong></p>\n\n<p>With Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, you can transform and analyze streaming data in real time with Apache Flink. Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink takes care of everything required to continuously run streaming applications and scales automatically to match the volume and throughput of your incoming data. Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink supports all operators from Apache Flink that can be used to solve a wide variety of use cases including map, KeyBy, aggregations, windows, joins, and more. Aggregations performs processing across multiple keys such as sum, min, and max. Window Join joins two data streams together on a given key and window.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c994021e76b1ecad390fff6f536e0fa5d0a77bf6e3aa0c9d351a8e0e052d81dc.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/c994021e76b1ecad390fff6f536e0fa5d0a77bf6e3aa0c9d351a8e0e052d81dc.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/faqs/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>For the given use case, Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is the best fit to perform real-time analytics including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes with high fault tolerance, since this solution involves minimal operation overhead.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes</strong> - A Kinesis data stream is a set of shards. Each shard contains a sequence of data records. A consumer is an application that processes the data from a Kinesis data stream. You can map a Lambda function to a shared-throughput consumer (standard iterator), or to a dedicated-throughput consumer with enhanced fan-out. For this option, you will need to integrate a Lambda function as a consumer and develop custom code to perform time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes. This represents additional operational overhead, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer app running on an EC2 instance to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes</strong> - A Kinesis data stream is a set of shards. Each shard contains a sequence of data records. A consumer is an application that processes the data from a Kinesis data stream. You can set up a consumer app on an EC2 instance. However, you will need to develop custom code to perform time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes. In addition, you will need to monitor and maintain the EC2 instance. So, this option represents additional operational overhead and hence it is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage Amazon Kinesis Firehose with a data transformation Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes</strong> - Amazon Kinesis Firehose can only be used for near real-time analysis and NOT real-time analysis, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/faqs/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/firehose/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/firehose/faqs/</a></p>"},{"id":53,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A business is moving their data to Amazon Redshift. A core table with billions of rows needs to be moved to Redshift. This table contains certain columns that have sensitive data that can only be accessed by the finance team. Once the data is moved to Redshift, queries will be run on this table by multiple teams.</p>\n\n<p>How will you configure a solution for this requirement such that the columns holding sensitive data are only accessible to members of the finance team?</p>","answers":["<p>Grant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Use the GRANT SQL command to allow read-only access to a subset of columns having non-sensitive data to the other users</p>","<p>Grant the finance group permission to read from the table. Create a view of the new table with only those columns having non-sensitive data. Grant the other users read-only permissions to this view</p>","<p>Grant all users read-only permissions to the non-sensitive columns. Add the finance team to the administrator group so they have complete access to the table</p>","<p>Grant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Create a second table having data only for columns with non-sensitive data. Grant read-only permissions to the second table for the rest of the users</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Grant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Use the GRANT SQL command to allow read-only access to a subset of columns having non-sensitive data to the other users</strong></p>\n\n<p>Since March 2020, Amazon Redshift supports column-level access control for data in Redshift. Customers can use column-level GRANT and REVOKE statements to help meet their security and compliance needs.</p>\n\n<p>Redshift's table-level access controls for the data in Redshift are already in use by many customers, but they also want the ability to control access in more detail. You can now control access to columns without having to implement view-based access control or use another system. Column-level access control is available in all Amazon Redshift regions.</p>\n\n<p>GRANT command defines access privileges for a user or user group. Privileges include access options such as being able to read data in tables and views, write data, create tables, and drop tables. Use this command to give specific privileges for a table, database, schema, function, procedure, language, or column.</p>\n\n<p>The syntax for column-level privileges on Amazon Redshift tables and views looks like the below:</p>\n\n<pre><code>GRANT { { SELECT | UPDATE } ( column_name [, ...] ) [, ...] | ALL [ PRIVILEGES ] ( column_name [,...] ) }\n    ON { [ TABLE ] table_name [, ...] }\n    TO { username | GROUP group_name | PUBLIC } [, ...]\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Grant all users read-only permissions to the non-sensitive columns. Add the finance team to the administrator group so they have complete access to the table</strong> - This is a security concern as the finance team do not need access privileges as an administrator.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Grant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Create a second table having data only for columns with non-sensitive data. Grant read-only permissions to the second table for the rest of the users</strong> - This is not an efficient solution as the storage space would be wasted to create the second table. It is better to use column-level access controls on the original table.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Grant the finance group permission to read from the table. Create a view of the new table with only those columns having non-sensitive data. Grant the other users read-only permissions to this view</strong> - AWS recommends that you should use column-level access control, instead of views, to manage access to sensitive columns within a table.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html#r_GRANT-usage-notes-clp\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html#r_GRANT-usage-notes-clp</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2020/03/announcing-column-level-access-control-for-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2020/03/announcing-column-level-access-control-for-amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":54,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A company wants to store data from Athena CTAS (<code>CREATE TABLE AS SELECT</code>) query results in Amazon S3. A data engineer wants to understand the distinction between partitioning vs bucketing for storing data via such CTAS queries.</p>\n\n<p>As an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, which of the following options would you identify as the right fit for choosing bucketing vs partitioning? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>Bucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has low cardinality and evenly distributed values</p>","<p>Bucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and sparsely distributed values</p>","<p>Bucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and evenly distributed values</p>","<p>Partitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have low cardinality</p>","<p>Partitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have high cardinality</p>"],"correct":[2,3],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Partitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have low cardinality</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Bucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and evenly distributed values</strong></p>\n\n<p>By partitioning your data, you can restrict the amount of data scanned by each query, thus improving performance and reducing cost. You can partition your data by any key. A common practice is to partition the data based on time, often leading to a multi-level partitioning scheme. For example, a customer who has data coming in every hour might decide to partition by year, month, date, and hour. Another customer, who has data coming from many different sources but that is loaded only once per day, might partition by a data source identifier and date.</p>\n\n<p>When you run a CTAS query, Athena writes the results to a specified location in Amazon S3. If you specify partitions, it creates them and stores each partition in a separate partition folder in the same location. Having partitions in Amazon S3 helps with Athena query performance because this helps you run targeted queries for only specific partitions. As a best practice, you should partition data by those columns that have similar characteristics, such as records from the same department, and that can have a limited number of possible values (low cardinality), such as a limited number of distinct departments in an organization.</p>\n\n<p>Bucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and evenly distributed values. For example, columns storing timestamp data could potentially have a very large number of distinct values, and their data is evenly distributed across the data set. To choose the column by which to bucket the CTAS query results, use the column that has a high number of values (high cardinality) and whose data can be split for storage into many buckets that will have roughly the same amount of data. Columns that are sparsely populated with values are not good candidates for bucketing.</p>\n\n<p>You should also note that you can partition and use bucketing for storing the results of the same CTAS query. These techniques for writing data do not exclude each other. Typically, the columns you use for bucketing differ from those you use for partitioning.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c4726e2184840cb11b127fad8bada0f9d6ad45b7015dda0a6f1862dd7b27018c.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/c4726e2184840cb11b127fad8bada0f9d6ad45b7015dda0a6f1862dd7b27018c.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/bucketing-vs-partitioning.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/bucketing-vs-partitioning.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Partitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have high cardinality</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Bucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has low cardinality and evenly distributed values</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Bucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and sparsely distributed values</strong></p>\n\n<p>These three options contradict the explanation provided above, so these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/bucketing-vs-partitioning.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/bucketing-vs-partitioning.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html</a></p>"},{"id":55,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A healthcare analytics company is building a serverless, multi-stage data processing pipeline in AWS. The pipeline reads raw claims data from an Amazon S3 data lake, transforms the data by using several AWS Glue ETL jobs, and loads the curated results into an Amazon Redshift database. A data engineer must orchestrate the workflow, manage dependencies between stages, and keep the pipeline serverless.</p>\n\n<p>Which solutions will meet these requirements? (Choose TWO.)</p>","answers":["<p>Use AWS CodePipeline to coordinate the ETL workflow and infrastructure deployments, and define the AWS Glue ETL job dependencies as pipeline stages</p>","<p>Use AWS Glue workflows to create a visual graph of the ETL jobs, triggers, and dependencies that run the multi-step data processing pipeline</p>","<p>Use AWS Step Functions to coordinate the AWS Glue ETL jobs, load stages, and dependency logic, and configure retry and error handling behavior in the state machine</p>","<p>Use Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that coordinates the AWS Glue ETL jobs and Redshift load tasks on a managed Airflow environment</p>","<p>Use Amazon EventBridge rules to start AWS Glue ETL jobs when new S3 objects arrive, chain additional EventBridge rules for later stages, and manage complex dependencies across rules</p>"],"correct":[1,2],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Step Functions to coordinate the AWS Glue ETL jobs, load stages, and dependency logic, and configure retry and error handling behavior in the state machine</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Step Functions is a serverless orchestration service that can coordinate AWS Glue jobs as part of a state machine. The data engineer can model each pipeline stage as a state, invoke AWS Glue ETL jobs, and move to the next stage only when the previous step completes successfully.</p>\n\n<p>Step Functions also supports workflow-level error handling. The data engineer can define Retry and Catch behavior to handle job failures, transient service errors, and fallback paths without writing a custom orchestration framework.</p>\n\n<p>This solution fits the requirement because the pipeline is multi-step and serverless. Step Functions keeps orchestration separate from ETL code, which makes the dependencies, retries, failure handling, and Redshift load sequence easier to manage and monitor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue workflows to create a visual graph of the ETL jobs, triggers, and dependencies that run the multi-step data processing pipeline</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue workflows are designed to orchestrate complex ETL activities that involve AWS Glue jobs, crawlers, and triggers. A workflow can represent the relationships between these components as a graph, which helps the data engineer visualize dependencies across the pipeline.</p>\n\n<p>Glue workflows can run jobs on demand, on a schedule, or through triggers. This makes them suitable for coordinating multiple Glue ETL jobs that extract data from Amazon S3, transform the data, and prepare downstream loads into Amazon Redshift.</p>\n\n<p>This solution also remains serverless because AWS Glue manages the ETL execution environment and workflow orchestration components. The data engineer does not need to provision orchestration servers or build a custom dependency engine.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c5977a6d0a5eb6e96de0c1325bc4d08549ff77908523cffadacf65137bd6ea13.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/c5977a6d0a5eb6e96de0c1325bc4d08549ff77908523cffadacf65137bd6ea13.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/f531e622ef07640fffc44bdd4cbca5ab03b855be1dc6a3cf9cae87656af1ed49.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/f531e622ef07640fffc44bdd4cbca5ab03b855be1dc6a3cf9cae87656af1ed49.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon EventBridge rules to start AWS Glue ETL jobs when new S3 objects arrive, chain additional EventBridge rules for later stages, and manage complex dependencies across rules</strong> - Amazon EventBridge can respond to S3 object creation events and can invoke AWS Glue workflows or jobs. It is useful for event-driven pipeline starts and service event routing.</p>\n\n<p>However, chaining many EventBridge rules to manage complex ETL dependencies is not the best orchestration pattern. EventBridge rules do not provide the same end-to-end workflow graph, dependency management, and coordinated retry structure that Step Functions or AWS Glue workflows provide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS CodePipeline to coordinate the ETL workflow and infrastructure deployments, and define the AWS Glue ETL job dependencies as pipeline stages</strong> - AWS CodePipeline orchestrates software release workflows, such as source, build, test, and deployment stages. It can coordinate infrastructure and application delivery activities.</p>\n\n<p>This ETL requirement focuses on data pipeline orchestration, not continuous delivery of application or infrastructure changes. CodePipeline is not the right service to manage recurring data transformation dependencies across Glue jobs and Redshift loads.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that coordinates the AWS Glue ETL jobs and Redshift load tasks on a managed Airflow environment</strong> - Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow can orchestrate data pipelines and can coordinate AWS Glue jobs. It is a managed service for Apache Airflow and can be appropriate for teams that already use Airflow DAGs.</p>\n\n<p>However, the question asks for serverless ETL orchestration with the least direct fit among the given choices. Step Functions and AWS Glue workflows provide AWS-native serverless orchestration for Glue-centric pipelines with less platform complexity than maintaining Airflow DAGs and an MWAA environment.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-amazon-redshift-based-etl-workflows-with-aws-step-functions-and-aws-glue/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-amazon-redshift-based-etl-workflows-with-aws-step-functions-and-aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":56,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A financial technology company is building an application that must search and analyze payment activity data in near real time. The application must support up to 1,200 search and analytics requests each second while keeping query latency low. The company wants each data product team to own its environment and tune storage, indexing, and query performance based on that team's budget and workload requirements.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use AWS Glue jobs to transform the payment activity data into Apache Parquet files in Amazon S3, catalog the files in AWS Glue Data Catalog, and assign each data team a separate Athena workgroup for analysis</p>","<p>Use Amazon OpenSearch Service domains with indexes to search and analyze the data, and assign each data team a separate domain to configure storage and query performance</p>","<p>Use Amazon Aurora clusters with Aurora I/O-Optimized configuration to store the data, and assign each data team a separate Aurora cluster</p>","<p>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to process the data, and assign each data team a separate stream to consume</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon OpenSearch Service domains with indexes to search and analyze the data, and assign each data team a separate domain to configure storage and query performance</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon OpenSearch Service is designed for search, log analytics, real-time application monitoring, clickstream analysis, and other low-latency search and analytics workloads. It uses indexes so applications can retrieve and analyze data quickly without scanning large raw datasets for every request.</p>\n\n<p>The requirement includes up to 1,200 requests each second with low query latency. OpenSearch Service domains can be sized and tuned for indexing throughput, search throughput, storage, shard strategy, and node configuration. This makes the service a strong fit for high-throughput near real-time search and analysis.</p>\n\n<p>Assigning each data team a separate OpenSearch Service domain gives each team ownership of its cost and performance settings. Each team can tune instance types, storage, indexes, shards, replicas, retention policies, and query patterns without affecting other teams that have different workload profiles.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ecc8741aba35dfda0cf31bb4980312b21ca59e45a90cd3f9f64e2f288e939b1a.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/ecc8741aba35dfda0cf31bb4980312b21ca59e45a90cd3f9f64e2f288e939b1a.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/d2a190554ee0098d36f83308361d63680d21dbd1c8f3ce7816190f688a678719.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/d2a190554ee0098d36f83308361d63680d21dbd1c8f3ce7816190f688a678719.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue jobs to transform the payment activity data into Apache Parquet files in Amazon S3, catalog the files in AWS Glue Data Catalog, and assign each data team a separate Athena workgroup for analysis</strong> - AWS Glue, Parquet, the AWS Glue Data Catalog, and Athena can create an efficient serverless analytics architecture for S3 data lakes. This approach can reduce scanned data and improve query cost for batch or interactive analytics.</p>\n\n<p>However, this architecture is not optimized for high-frequency, low-latency application search. It also depends on file transformation and query execution workflows rather than indexing data for near real-time search and analysis.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to process the data, and assign each data team a separate stream to consume</strong> - Kinesis Data Streams and Managed Service for Apache Flink can process streaming data in real time. This combination is suitable for stream processing, windowed aggregations, stateful transformations, and continuous analytics pipelines.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option does not provide a search index that applications can query with low latency at high request rates. It processes streaming events, but it does not directly satisfy the application's need for near real-time search and analysis across indexed data.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Aurora clusters with Aurora I/O-Optimized configuration to store the data, and assign each data team a separate Aurora cluster</strong> - Amazon Aurora can support transactional and relational workloads with high performance. Aurora I/O-Optimized can help workloads that have high I/O usage and require predictable database pricing.</p>\n\n<p>However, Aurora is not the best fit for near real-time search and analytics across large volumes of indexed application data. The requirement focuses on search and analysis with low query latency, which aligns more closely with OpenSearch Service.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/indexing.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/indexing.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/run-log-analytics-for-a-fraction-of-the-cost-with-the-new-engine-for-amazon-opensearch-service/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/run-log-analytics-for-a-fraction-of-the-cost-with-the-new-engine-for-amazon-opensearch-service/</a></p>"},{"id":57,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>An HTTP application is deployed on an Auto Scaling Group, is accessible from an Application Load Balancer (ALB) that provides HTTPS termination and accesses a PostgreSQL database managed by Amazon RDS.</p>\n\n<p>How should you configure the security groups? (Select three)</p>","answers":["<p>The security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 80</p>","<p>The security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 5432</p>","<p>The security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Application Load Balancer on port 80</p>","<p>The security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 443</p>","<p>The security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 80</p>","<p>The security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon RDS database on port 5432</p>"],"correct":[1,2,3],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>The security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 5432</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>The security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Application Load Balancer on port 80</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>The security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 443</strong></p>\n\n<p>A security group acts as a virtual firewall that controls the traffic for one or more instances. When you launch an instance, you can specify one or more security groups; otherwise, we use the default security group. You can add rules to each security group that allows traffic to or from its associated instances. You can modify the rules for a security group at any time; the new rules are automatically applied to all instances that are associated with the security group. When we decide whether to allow traffic to reach an instance, we evaluate all the rules from all the security groups that are associated with the instance.</p>\n\n<p>The following are the characteristics of security group rules:\n1. By default, security groups allow all outbound traffic.\n2. Security group rules are always permissive; you can't create rules that deny access.\n3. Security groups are stateful</p>\n\n<p>PostgreSQL port = 5432\nHTTP port = 80\nHTTPS port = 443</p>\n\n<p>The traffic goes like this :\nThe client sends an HTTPS request to ALB on port 443. This is handled by the rule - \"The security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 443\"</p>\n\n<p>The Application Load Balancer then forwards the request to one of the Amazon EC2 instances. This is handled by the rule - \"The security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Application Load Balancer on port 80\"</p>\n\n<p>The Amazon EC2 instance further accesses the PostgreSQL database managed by Amazon RDS on port 5432. This is handled by the rule - \"The security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 5432\"</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>The security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 80</strong> - The client sends an HTTPS request to ALB on port 443 and not on port 80, so this is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon RDS database on port 5432</strong> - The security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Application Load Balancer and not from the security group of the Amazon RDS database, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 80</strong> - The Amazon EC2 instance further accesses the PostgreSQL database managed by Amazon RDS on port 5432 and not on port 80, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-security-groups.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-security-groups.html</a></p>"},{"id":58,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A payment processing company receives a daily CSV file in an Amazon S3 bucket. The file contains several million settlement records that must be validated before the company updates a transaction database. The data engineering team needs a serverless solution that processes the records in parallel. The workflow must stop the entire processing run if more than 12% of the records fail validation.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Run an AWS Batch array job that divides the CSV file into chunks, validates the chunks in parallel, and uses custom code to track failed records across all child jobs</p>","<p>Use an AWS Step Functions Distributed Map state to process the CSV records in parallel and set ToleratedFailurePercentage to 12</p>","<p>Deploy an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark to process the CSV file in parallel and configure custom Spark logic to stop the job when failed validations exceed 12%</p>","<p>Create an AWS Glue job that reads the CSV file from Amazon S3, repartitions the data in Spark, validates the records, and uses a custom accumulator to stop processing when the failed-record percentage exceeds 12%</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use an AWS Step Functions Distributed Map state to process the CSV records in parallel and set ToleratedFailurePercentage to 12</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Step Functions Distributed Map is designed for large-scale parallel workloads. It can read a CSV file from Amazon S3 by using an ItemReader and can process each row through child workflow executions, which fits the requirement to validate millions of transaction records in parallel.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2188722553d1cbd5aa61cd55c67a5f64eddc2c365a165f1b1f212a206afa9814.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/2188722553d1cbd5aa61cd55c67a5f64eddc2c365a165f1b1f212a206afa9814.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map-distributed.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map-distributed.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Distributed Map supports failure thresholds by count or percentage. The data engineer can set ToleratedFailurePercentage to 12 so the Map Run fails automatically when failed item processing exceeds the allowed threshold. This directly satisfies the requirement to stop the entire process when more than 12% of records fail validation.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational overhead because Step Functions provides managed orchestration, parallelization, execution tracking, and failure-threshold handling. The company does not need to run clusters, build a custom distributed counter, manage queue fan-out logic, or maintain custom job-control code.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/fd720b511c1ac07aa8ec9f63a2fa723f2ee83c39a267c94f54c8b68e9376acdf.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/fd720b511c1ac07aa8ec9f63a2fa723f2ee83c39a267c94f54c8b68e9376acdf.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/d5f249ee36522f511fd7644b3ec5e0603979eaa569b318fd9469d347a8eac5ab.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/d5f249ee36522f511fd7644b3ec5e0603979eaa569b318fd9469d347a8eac5ab.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run an AWS Batch array job that divides the CSV file into chunks, validates the chunks in parallel, and uses custom code to track failed records across all child jobs</strong> - AWS Batch can run parallel workloads, and an array job can process chunks of a large file. However, the team must build and maintain chunking logic, job coordination, error aggregation, and the global failure-threshold mechanism.</p>\n\n<p>This option adds more operational overhead than Step Functions Distributed Map. The requirement is serverless-style orchestration with built-in failure threshold handling, which Distributed Map provides directly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Deploy an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark to process the CSV file in parallel and configure custom Spark logic to stop the job when failed validations exceed 12%</strong> - Amazon EMR with Apache Spark can process large CSV files in parallel. Spark can validate records and can implement custom logic to calculate the failure percentage.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option requires the company to configure, scale, monitor, and manage an EMR cluster or EMR job runtime. It also requires custom failure-threshold logic, which adds overhead compared with using a managed Distributed Map field.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an AWS Glue job that reads the CSV file from Amazon S3, repartitions the data in Spark, validates the records, and uses a custom accumulator to stop processing when the failed-record percentage exceeds 12%</strong> - AWS Glue can run Spark ETL jobs and can process large files from Amazon S3. It can also repartition data to improve parallel processing.</p>\n\n<p>However, the failure-threshold behavior would require custom Spark logic and careful job control. Step Functions Distributed Map provides the parallel record processing and tolerated failure percentage setting with less custom code.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map-distributed.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map-distributed.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/input-output-itemreader.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/input-output-itemreader.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/</a></p>"},{"id":59,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>An Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) job needs to be implemented which will process data uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket daily. The uploaded data is in the form of .csv files, each of which is around 100 MB in size.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following represents the most cost-effective solution for the ETL job?</p>","answers":["<p>Run the transformations on the data using AWS Glue DataBrew</p>","<p>Write an AWS Glue PySpark job. Use Apache Spark to transform the data</p>","<p>Configure an AWS Glue Python shell job. Use the pre-loaded Pandas library to run transformations on the data</p>","<p>Run the transformations on the data using AWS Glue Data Studio</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure an AWS Glue Python shell job. Use the pre-loaded Pandas library to run transformations on the data</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue ETL supports running plain non-distributed Python scripts as a shell script to run small to medium-sized generic tasks that are often part of an ETL workflow. For example, to submit SQL queries to services such as Amazon Redshift, Amazon Athena, or Amazon EMR, or run machine learning (ML) and scientific analyses.</p>\n\n<p>You can run Python shell jobs using one Data Processing Unit (DPU) or 0.0625 DPU (which is 1/16 DPU), allowing you to run cost-effective small to medium jobs that do not require Spark runtime.</p>\n\n<p>Compared to AWS Lambda, which has a strict 15-minute maximum timeout, AWS Glue Python Shell can be configured with a much longer timeout and higher memory, often required for data engineering jobs.</p>\n\n<p>Python shell jobs in AWS Glue come pre-loaded with libraries such as Boto3, NumPy, SciPy, Pandas, and others.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Write an AWS Glue PySpark job. Use Apache Spark to transform the data</strong> - The AWS Glue PySpark job can certainly be used for the ETL job, however, it is a costlier solution than just using the AWS Glue Python shell job (with Pandas library) for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run the transformations on the data using AWS Glue DataBrew</strong> - AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that makes it easier for data analysts and data scientists to clean and normalize data to prepare it for analytics and machine learning (ML). AWS Glue DataBrew is not an ETL solution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run the transformations on the data using AWS Glue Data Studio</strong> - AWS Glue Studio is a graphical interface that makes it easy to create, run, and monitor data integration jobs in AWS Glue. You can visually compose data transformation workflows and seamlessly run them on the Apache Spark-based serverless ETL engine in AWS Glue. Glue Data Studio is not an ETL solution.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job-python.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job-python.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/additional-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/additional-considerations.html</a></p>"},{"id":60,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>The HR department at a company wants to process employees data stored in Amazon S3 in the Microsoft Excel worksheet format. The data has the following column names: id, name, email, and phone. The department wants to create a single column to store these values in the following format:</p>\n\n<pre><code>{\n    \"id\": 1,\n    \"name\": \"John Doe\",\n    \"email\": \"johndoe@example.com\",\n    \"phone\": \"9876543210\"\n}\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Which of the following options will meet this requirement with the LEAST coding effort?</p>","answers":["<p>Process the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_ARRAY transformation to create the new column</p>","<p>Process the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_MAP transformation to create the new column</p>","<p>Process the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_parse function to create the new column</p>","<p>Process the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_format function to create the new column</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Process the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_MAP transformation to create the new column</strong></p>\n\n<p>In DataBrew, a recipe step is an action that transforms your raw data into a form that is ready to be consumed by your data pipeline. A DataBrew function is a special kind of recipe step that performs a computation based on parameters.</p>\n\n<p>You can use the NEST_TO_MAP recipe step to convert user-selected columns into key-value pairs, each with a key representing the column name and a value representing the row value. The order of the selected column is not maintained while creating the resultant map.</p>\n\n<p>Example:</p>\n\n<pre><code>{\n    \"RecipeAction\": {\n        \"Operation\": \"NEST_TO_MAP\",\n        \"Parameters\": {\n            \"sourceColumns\": \"[\"age\",\"weight_kg\",\"height_cm\"]\",\n            \"targetColumn\": \"columnName\",\n            \"removeSourceColumns\": \"true\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Process the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_format function to create the new column</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Process the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_parse function to create the new column</strong></p>\n\n<p>The function json_format returns the JSON text serialized from the input JSON value.</p>\n\n<p>The function json_parse returns the JSON value deserialized from the input JSON text.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/009bcc11e9a7fcd8f753cc4836ca146d5090a8ec2e605cf5d34a670d9f3d6b48.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/009bcc11e9a7fcd8f753cc4836ca146d5090a8ec2e605cf5d34a670d9f3d6b48.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://prestodb.io/docs/current/functions/json.html\">https://prestodb.io/docs/current/functions/json.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Athena supports creating tables and querying data from CSV, TSV, custom-delimited, and JSON formats; data from Hadoop-related formats: ORC, Apache Avro, and Parquet; logs from Logstash, AWS CloudTrail logs, and Apache WebServer logs. Athena does not support querying data from files stored in the Microsoft Excel workbook format, so both these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Process the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_ARRAY transformation to create the new column</strong> - You can use the NEST_TO_ARRAY recipe step to convert user-selected columns into array values. The order of the selected columns is maintained while creating the resultant array. The different column data types are typecast to a common type that supports the data types of all columns. Since the output is an array and not a JSON, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/supported-data-file-sources.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/supported-data-file-sources.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/supported-serdes.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/supported-serdes.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.NEST_TO_MAP.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.NEST_TO_MAP.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.NEST_TO_ARRAY.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.NEST_TO_ARRAY.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://prestodb.io/docs/current/functions/json.html\">https://prestodb.io/docs/current/functions/json.html</a></p>"},{"id":61,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A healthcare startup needs to enforce compliance and regulatory guidelines for objects stored in Amazon S3. One of the key requirements is to provide adequate protection against accidental deletion of objects.</p>\n\n<p>What are your recommendations to address these guidelines? (Select two) ?</p>","answers":["<p>Establish a process to get managerial approval for deleting Amazon S3 objects</p>","<p>Create an event trigger on deleting any Amazon S3 object. The event invokes an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) notification via email to the IT manager</p>","<p>Enable multi-factor authentication (MFA) delete on the Amazon S3 bucket</p>","<p>Change the configuration on the Amazon S3 console so that the user needs to provide additional confirmation while deleting any Amazon S3 object</p>","<p>Enable versioning on the Amazon S3 bucket</p>"],"correct":[2,4],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable versioning on the Amazon S3 bucket</strong></p>\n\n<p>Versioning is a means of keeping multiple variants of an object in the same bucket. You can use versioning to preserve, retrieve, and restore every version of every object stored in your Amazon S3 bucket.\nVersioning-enabled buckets enable you to recover objects from accidental deletion or overwrite.</p>\n\n<p>For example:</p>\n\n<p>If you overwrite an object, it results in a new object version in the bucket. You can always restore the previous version.\nIf you delete an object, instead of removing it permanently, Amazon S3 inserts a delete marker, which becomes the current object version. You can always restore the previous version. Hence, this is the correct option.</p>\n\n<p>Versioning Overview:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/b55f2798b482bff78730025acd0e36ca814819d23f0b1ac93b8e22d4d98a6c32.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/b55f2798b482bff78730025acd0e36ca814819d23f0b1ac93b8e22d4d98a6c32.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/Versioning.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/Versioning.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable multi-factor authentication (MFA) delete on the Amazon S3 bucket</strong></p>\n\n<p>To provide additional protection, multi-factor authentication (MFA) delete can be enabled. MFA delete requires secondary authentication to take place before objects can be permanently deleted from an Amazon S3 bucket. Hence, this is the correct option.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an event trigger on deleting any Amazon S3 object. The event invokes an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) notification via email to the IT manager</strong> - Sending an event trigger after object deletion does not meet the objective of preventing object deletion by mistake because the object has already been deleted. So, this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Establish a process to get managerial approval for deleting Amazon S3 objects</strong> - This option for getting managerial approval is just a distractor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Change the configuration on the Amazon S3 console so that the user needs to provide additional confirmation while deleting any Amazon S3 object</strong> - There is no provision to set up Amazon S3 configuration to ask for additional confirmation before deleting an object. This option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/Versioning.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/Versioning.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/UsingMFADelete.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/UsingMFADelete.html</a></p>"},{"id":62,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A company maintains its datasets in JSON and .csv formats in an Amazon S3 bucket and utilizes Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server, Amazon DynamoDB (in provisioned capacity mode), and an Amazon Redshift cluster. The data engineering team is tasked with creating a solution that enables data scientists to query all these data sources using an SQL-like syntax.</p>\n\n<p>What solution would fulfill these requirements while incurring the least operational overhead and minimizing the costs?</p>","answers":["<p>Leverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform the JSON data to .csv format and query the resultant data in .csv format using Amazon Athena</p>","<p>Leverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform both the JSON and .csv format data to parquet format and query the resultant data using Amazon Athena</p>","<p>Leverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Athena for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format</p>","<p>Leverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Athena for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format</strong></p>\n\n<p>The AWS Glue Data Catalog contains references to data that is used as sources and targets of your extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs in AWS Glue.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/35dff286e1196603004856680134250535b74ddde449855025ae4b76b7de6279.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/35dff286e1196603004856680134250535b74ddde449855025ae4b76b7de6279.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>If you have data in sources other than Amazon S3, you can use Athena Federated Query to query the data in place or build pipelines that extract data from multiple data sources and store them in Amazon S3. With Athena Federated Query, you can run SQL queries across data stored in relational, non-relational, object, and custom data sources.</p>\n\n<p>Athena uses data source connectors that run on AWS Lambda to run federated queries. A data source connector is a piece of code that can translate between your target data source and Athena. You can think of a connector as an extension of Athena's query engine. Prebuilt Athena data source connectors exist for data sources like Amazon CloudWatch Logs, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon DocumentDB, and Amazon RDS, and JDBC-compliant relational data sources such MySQL, and PostgreSQL under the Apache 2.0 license.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Athena also allows federated query pass-through, which allows entire queries to be executed directly on the underlying data source. Federated query pass-through allows you to take advantage of the unique functions, query language, and performance capabilities of different data sources, and can result in faster query execution and less data processed by Athena. You can run Athena queries on DynamoDB using the PartiQL language. Federated query pass-through is also useful when you want to run SELECT queries that aggregate, join, or invoke functions of your data source that are not available in Athena.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format</strong> - Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data would involve additional overhead of setting up Redshift spectrum on the existing Redshift cluster. Redshift Spectrum would also turn out to be costlier than Amazon Athena, as it leverages the resources of the Redshift cluster.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform the JSON data to .csv format and query the resultant data in .csv format using Amazon Athena</strong> - Using AWS Glue jobs to transform the JSON data to .csv format represents unnecessary work to build a solution, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform both the JSON and .csv format data to parquet format and query the resultant data using Amazon Athena</strong> - Using AWS Glue jobs to transform both the JSON and .csv format data to parquet format represents unnecessary work to build a solution, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2024/04/amazon-athena-federated-query-pass-through/\">https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2024/04/amazon-athena-federated-query-pass-through/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html</a></p>"},{"id":63,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A media analytics company needs to reduce storage costs for an Amazon S3 bucket that receives 8 million event objects every day. The objects range from 4 KB to 6 MB. The objects must remain immediately accessible for the first 45 days. Users access the objects infrequently from day 46 through day 150. From day 151 through day 400, the objects must remain retrievable within an hour. The company can delete the objects after 400 days.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Enable S3 Inventory and run a daily Lambda function to transition objects between storage classes and delete objects after 400 days</p>","<p>Use S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Access tier enabled and configure an S3 bucket policy to delete objects that are older than 400 days</p>","<p>Create an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 One Zone-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days</p>","<p>Create an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days</strong></p>\n\n<p>S3 Lifecycle policies are designed to transition objects between storage classes and expire objects based on object age. This makes Lifecycle the most operationally efficient mechanism for a predictable retention and access pattern.</p>\n\n<p>The objects need immediate access for the first 45 days, so S3 Standard is appropriate during the initial active-access period. From day 46 through day 150, users access the objects infrequently, so S3 Standard-IA matches the requirement for immediate access with lower storage cost for infrequently accessed data.</p>\n\n<p>From day 151 through day 400, the objects must be retrievable within an hour. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval supports archive storage with retrieval options that can meet this requirement. The same S3 Lifecycle rule can expire the objects after 400 days, which avoids custom deletion automation.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/126c73f6693ff877a637379e29032e2760bf90cddbfe0cad32de3c9cc8ae0291.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/126c73f6693ff877a637379e29032e2760bf90cddbfe0cad32de3c9cc8ae0291.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0a0d47bb5d14db39cd7ab5e483692577fef89e496ce6dcaec710851d61484957.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/0a0d47bb5d14db39cd7ab5e483692577fef89e496ce6dcaec710851d61484957.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Access tier enabled and configure an S3 bucket policy to delete objects that are older than 400 days</strong> - S3 Intelligent-Tiering can optimize storage costs when access patterns are unknown or changing. It can also use optional archive access tiers for objects that become rarely accessed.</p>\n\n<p>However, this scenario has a known access pattern with specific day ranges. Also, an S3 bucket policy does not expire objects based on age. S3 Lifecycle expiration is the correct mechanism for deleting objects after the retention period.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable S3 Inventory and run a daily Lambda function to transition objects between storage classes and delete objects after 400 days</strong> - S3 Inventory can list objects and a Lambda function can use that information to move or delete objects. This design could be built to follow the required timing rules.</p>\n\n<p>However, it requires custom code, daily scheduling, retry handling, and operational monitoring. S3 Lifecycle provides native object transition and expiration controls with much less maintenance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 One Zone-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days</strong> - S3 One Zone-IA can reduce storage cost for infrequently accessed data that does not require storage across multiple Availability Zones. S3 Glacier Deep Archive can reduce archive storage cost further.</p>\n\n<p>However, S3 One Zone-IA is not the best fit when the company needs resilient storage for large operational datasets. S3 Glacier Deep Archive also does not meet the requirement to retrieve objects within an hour.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/restoring-objects-retrieval-options.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/restoring-objects-retrieval-options.html</a></p>"},{"id":64,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A retail analytics company uses an AWS Glue PySpark job to enrich order data with selected customer profile records from an Amazon DynamoDB table. The job already knows the partition key values for the customer records that it needs to retrieve. The existing transformation logic requires the retrieved customer records to be available as an AWS Glue DynamicFrame. The company must ensure that the AWS Glue job reads only the required customer records from DynamoDB while consuming the fewest possible read capacity units.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Create a DynamoDB global secondary index on the sort key, scan the index from the AWS Glue job, and convert all matching index records into a DynamicFrame</p>","<p>Use the AWS Glue DynamoDB ETL connector to read the table, configure a filter for the customer partition keys, and load the filtered output as a DynamicFrame</p>","<p>Run DynamoDB Query operations from the AWS Glue job by using the partition key values in the key condition expression, then create a DynamicFrame from the returned items</p>","<p>Run a DynamoDB query from the AWS Glue job by using only the sort key in the key condition expression, then convert the results to a DynamicFrame</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run DynamoDB Query operations from the AWS Glue job by using the partition key values in the key condition expression, then create a DynamicFrame from the returned items</strong></p>\n\n<p>A DynamoDB Query operation is the most efficient way to retrieve known items when the application already knows the partition key values. The AWS Glue PySpark job can call DynamoDB Query for the required partition key values and retrieve only the items that match the key condition expression.</p>\n\n<p>This approach minimizes read capacity unit consumption because DynamoDB reads the targeted item collection for the specified partition key instead of reading the entire table. If the table also has a sort key, the job can optionally add a sort key condition to narrow the result set further.</p>\n\n<p>After the job retrieves the required DynamoDB items, the job can convert the returned records into a Spark DataFrame or RDD and then create an AWS Glue DynamicFrame. This satisfies the existing processing requirement while avoiding a table scan or post-read filtering pattern.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use the AWS Glue DynamoDB ETL connector to read the table, configure a filter for the customer partition keys, and load the filtered output as a DynamicFrame</strong> - The AWS Glue DynamoDB ETL connector can read data from a DynamoDB table into an AWS Glue DynamicFrame. This is useful when a job needs to ingest a full table or large portions of a table for ETL processing.</p>\n\n<p>However, using a filter after reading data does not provide the same RCU efficiency as a DynamoDB Query that uses the partition key in the key condition expression. To consume the minimum RCUs, the job must target the known partition key values during the DynamoDB read operation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run a DynamoDB query from the AWS Glue job by using only the sort key in the key condition expression, then convert the results to a DynamicFrame</strong> - A sort key can help narrow query results when the query already identifies the partition key. This is useful for retrieving a subset of items within the same partition.</p>\n\n<p>However, DynamoDB Query requires a partition key equality condition. A query that uses only the sort key in the key condition expression is not valid for directly retrieving records from the base table.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a DynamoDB global secondary index on the sort key, scan the index from the AWS Glue job, and convert all matching index records into a DynamicFrame</strong> - A global secondary index can support alternate access patterns when the application needs to query by attributes other than the base table partition key. This can be useful when the required lookup pattern is not supported by the table key design.</p>\n\n<p>However, the job already knows the base table partition key values. Scanning a secondary index by sort key is less efficient than querying the table directly by partition key, and it still risks reading many unnecessary index records.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/APIReference/API_Query.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/APIReference/API_Query.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Query.KeyConditionExpressions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Query.KeyConditionExpressions.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-extensions-dynamic-frame.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-extensions-dynamic-frame.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-dynamodb-home.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-dynamodb-home.html</a></p>"},{"id":65,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A fintech startup processes transaction logs and customer account records that are stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The bucket is encrypted using server-side encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS). Some of the stored data contains sensitive financial attributes such as customer bank account numbers, credit scores, and national ID numbers. To ensure data privacy and regulatory compliance, the company must automatically detect, mask, and control access to sensitive fields during the analytics workflow. Analysts need masked versions of the data for modeling, while certain pre-processing engineers require controlled access to unmasked data for enrichment tasks. The company wants a low-maintenance and scalable solution that ensures end-to-end protection of sensitive data within the pipeline.</p>\n\n<p>Which combination of solutions should the company use to fulfill these requirements? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>Define granular IAM policies that restrict access to sensitive S3 prefixes or objects based on user roles and job functions</p>","<p>Deploy Amazon Rekognition to analyze the structured financial records for PII and redact sensitive text before use in downstream services. Integrate this with a Lambda function to batch-process the data</p>","<p>Use AWS Glue DataBrew to identify and mask sensitive fields such as account numbers and national IDs using built-in transformations. Store the masked results in a new S3 bucket for downstream analytics</p>","<p>Enable Amazon Macie on the S3 bucket to automatically discover and classify sensitive data such as PII and financial data</p>","<p>Configure Amazon Detective to investigate potential unauthorized access to sensitive fields and generate detailed visualizations of user behavior on the data</p>"],"correct":[0,2],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue DataBrew to identify and mask sensitive fields such as account numbers and national IDs using built-in transformations. Store the masked results in a new S3 bucket for downstream analytics</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that enables data analysts and engineers to clean and transform data without writing code. It includes built-in transformations for masking, redacting, or hashing sensitive fields, such as social security numbers and account details. DataBrew integrates directly with Amazon S3 and supports scheduled jobs, making it a low-maintenance and scalable solution for applying masking logic before sensitive data is analyzed. It is well-suited for structured PII transformation tasks across the data pipeline.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/f925c1cb49db11b044b8024e012005a0c5576bb400071ef1e977ce289da7c55e.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/f925c1cb49db11b044b8024e012005a0c5576bb400071ef1e977ce289da7c55e.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/tw/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\">https://aws.amazon.com/tw/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Define granular IAM policies that restrict access to sensitive S3 prefixes or objects based on user roles and job functions</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS IAM is the standard and recommended approach to enforce fine-grained access control over AWS resources, including S3 buckets. In this context, IAM policies can be used to restrict access to unmasked PII data, granting permissions only to trusted users or services in the pre-processing phase. You can apply IAM conditions (e.g., restricting access via VPC endpoints, MFA, or encryption context) to ensure that only authorized identities can access or process sensitive data, aligning with compliance and least-privilege principles.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Deploy Amazon Rekognition to analyze the structured financial records for PII and redact sensitive text before use in downstream services. Integrate this with a Lambda function to batch-process the data</strong> - Amazon Rekognition is an AI service for image and video analysis, including facial recognition, object detection, and text detection in media. It is not designed to process structured data such as CSV or JSON containing financial records. Using Rekognition to scan S3 files with tabular PII would be impractical and misaligned with the service’s purpose.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure Amazon Detective to investigate potential unauthorized access to sensitive fields and generate detailed visualizations of user behavior on the data</strong> - Amazon Detective is designed for security investigations and threat analysis based on AWS CloudTrail, VPC flow logs, and GuardDuty findings. It helps visualize suspicious activity, but it does not inspect or monitor data content inside S3 buckets and cannot classify or mask PII. Hence, it's not suitable for protecting or masking sensitive data in analytics pipelines.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable Amazon Macie on the S3 bucket to automatically discover and classify sensitive data such as PII and financial data</strong> - Amazon Macie uses machine learning to discover and classify sensitive data in S3, such as PII and financial records. While it helps identify where sensitive data exists, it does not provide masking or access control features. Macie is useful as a discovery and classification tool, but by itself, it cannot enforce masking or protect access to PII in the engineering pipeline. For that, other services like DataBrew and IAM are needed. In the following reference blog, AWS recommends that you use Macie to analyze the objects and identify sensitive data using machine learning (ML) and pattern matching. Then you can use AWS Glue to run a workflow to anonymize or mask the data.</p>\n\n<p>Data masking and granular access control using Amazon Macie and AWS Lake Formation:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/73c338d01adbffafbbee4044bacae812f483ce0189bf53ae447d0639a445335e.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/73c338d01adbffafbbee4044bacae812f483ce0189bf53ae447d0639a445335e.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/data-masking-and-granular-access-control-using-amazon-macie-and-aws-lake-formation/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/data-masking-and-granular-access-control-using-amazon-macie-and-aws-lake-formation/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/tw/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\">https://aws.amazon.com/tw/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/data-masking-and-granular-access-control-using-amazon-macie-and-aws-lake-formation/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/data-masking-and-granular-access-control-using-amazon-macie-and-aws-lake-formation/</a></p>"}]};